{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LNXti6ae7ib6cBcX9hC2FR","type":"tool_result","content":"20\t14. three.js and Rapier: `RAPIER`, `THREE`, `TSL`\n21\t15. More: `applySky`, `bayer4`, `BehaviorTree`, `beltMaterial`, `BeltMaterial`, `BoidOptions`, `Boids`, `Branch`, `bt`, `BtNode`, `BtStatus`, `BulletPool`, `CameraKey`, `CameraShake`, `CameraShot`, `ClockState`, `coversPixel`, `createNoise`, `crystalMaterial`, `Cue`, `DECAL_SHAPES`, `DecalOptions`, `Decals`, `DecalShape`, `decodeState`, `drawSheet`, `ease`, `Ease`, `EASE_NAMES`, `EaseName`, `EASES`, `emitter`, `Emitter`, `encodeState`, `ErosionOptions`, `expand`, `FbmOptions`, `FlashOptions`, `forceFieldMaterial`, `GpuSwarm`, `GpuSwarmOptions`, `GrassField`, `GrassOptions`, `groundFog`, `hashString`, `HEADLESS_VIEWPORT`, `HeadlessView`, `HeadlessViewOptions`, `hologramMaterial`, `lavaMaterial`, `Leaf`, `LRule`, `LSystemDef`, `marbleMaterial`, `MAX_FRAMES`, `MAX_PUSHERS`, `MAX_WAVES`, `Mirror`, `MirrorOptions`, `NavGrid`, `NavGridOptions`, `Noise`, `Pattern`, `PatternDef`, `PatternTile`, `patternTiles`, `PLANTS`, `Precipitation`, `PrecipitationKind`, `PrecipitationOptions`, `Recording`, `recordingProblem`, `RenderView`, `RenderViewOptions`, `Rng`, `RopeMesh`, `SCREEN_LAYER`, `ScreenFx`, `ScreenFxState`, `ShaderOptions`, `ShakeState`, `ShockwaveOptions`, `skyAt`, `SkyState`, `Snapshot`, `snapshotProblem`, `SoftMesh`, `SpriteBatch`, `SpriteOptions`, `SpriteSheet`, `swayMaterial`, `swayObject`, `SwayOptions`, `Team`, `Terrain`, `TERRAIN_BANDS`, `TerrainBand`, `TerrainOptions`, `Timeline`, `TimelineLine`, `TimelineOptions`, `TraceRow`, `Trail`, `TrailOptions`, `TRANSITION_CELLS`, `TransitionKind`, `TransitionOptions`, `TRANSITIONS`, `turtle`, `TurtleOptions`, `Tween`, `TweenOptions`, `Tweens`, `View`, `WaterOptions`, `WaterSurface`, `Wfc`, `WfcOptions`, `WfcTile`, `WindUniforms`, `woodMaterial`\n22\t\n23\t=== class Engine @ 132\n24\t### class Engine\n25\t\n26\t- `static readonly version: string`: The engine's version (package.json).\n27\t- `static start(game: Game, options: EngineOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`\n28\t- `static headless(game: Game, options: HeadlessOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`: The engine without a page (docs/engine/headless.md): the same boot, game code and frames as `start`, with a `HeadlessView` that draws nothing, in Node. No DOM, no GPU, no listeners, no AudioContext (`audio.counts` still counts), no panels; errors go to `engin…\n29\t- `readonly errors: string[]`: Errors thrown by the game's hooks while the render loop ran, each message once, oldest first (at most 20). They are logged to the console and shown in a box on screen; the frame keeps rendering so the picture stays up. Fix the first one first. (`step()` throw…\n30\t- `readonly input: Input`\n31\t- `readonly sun: DirectionalLight`\n32\t- `readonly ambient: AmbientLight`\n33\t- `readonly context: GameContext`\n34\t- `readonly audio: AudioManager`\n35\t- `readonly particles: Particles`\n36\t- `readonly hud: Hud`\n37\t- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time (`ctx.tweens`).\n38\t- `readonly shake: CameraShake`: Screen shake (trauma 0..1): `engine.shake.add(0.4)`. Added to the camera after the rig placed it, on real time; ortho cameras move by whole art pixels. Reset on level unload.\n39\t- `debug: DebugUI | null`\n40\t- `touch: TouchControls | null`\n41\t- `readonly settings: Settings`: The player's settings: look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate and debug info (plain data, saved per game), applied over the game's own choices; `open()` shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button, `EngineOptions.settings`). Sound is `audio`'s.\n42\t- `debugKeys: DebugKeyMap`: Active engine hotkeys (from EngineOptions.debugKeys; none until the engine starts).\n43\t- `manual: boolean`: Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`. Frame-exact and independent of how fast the browser renders (filmstrips, replays).\n44\t- `frame: number`\n45\t- `fps: number`: Rendered frames per second (measured over ~0.5 s).\n46\t- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`: Requested quality (`auto` adapts); `quality` is the level in use.\n47\t- `time: number`\n48\t- `readonly renderer: View`\n49\t- `readonly physics: Physics`\n50\t- `camera: CameraRig`\n51\t- `readonly scene: Scene`\n52\t- `get gpu: PixelRenderer | null`: The page's renderer, for direct GPU work (readbacks, the canvas, compute): null headless (`Engine.headless`), where nothing is drawn. Gameplay reads `renderer`, which both have.\n53\t- `get game: Game`: The running game. Replace it with `loadGame()`.\n54\t- `get paused: boolean`: Freeze simulation, animation, particles and game time (rendering continues). For tooling, capture and pause menus. Queued key presses are dropped while paused.\n55\t- `get lights: LightPool`: The dynamic light pool (render/lights.ts), engine-owned: its lights stay in the scene across levels, so loading another level never changes the light count (no recompiles). Created on first use; sized by quality.\n56\t- `get timeScale: number`: Game speed: 1 normal, 0.25 slow motion, 2 double, 0 frozen (a menu: key presses made meanwhile are dropped, like a pause). Scales game time, physics, animation, particles, tweens and the camera follow; not transitions, shake or the render loop. Back to 1 when…\n57\t- `hitstop(seconds: number): void`: Freeze the game for `seconds` of real time: impact frames that sell a hit. Rendering, screen shake and transitions keep going; overlapping hitstops keep the longest. Key presses made during a hitstop still count afterwards (a buffered jump).\n58\t- `get screen: ScreenFx`: Transitions, flashes and shockwaves (render/screenFx.ts): `await engine.screen.cover('iris')`.\n59\t- `get sunDir: Vector3`: The direction sunlight comes from (unit, pointing at the sun).\n60\t- `setSunDirection(dir: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]): void`: Point the sun (and its shadows): a direction toward the sun, normalised here. A level's time of day or a sun dial; reset to the engine default when the level unloads. Below the horizon the sun is kept just above it (lower `sun.intensity` for night instead).\n61\t- `get quality: QualityLevel`: The quality level in use (see `EngineOptions.quality`).\n62\t- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`: Quality by option: a level, or `auto`: the device's level (low on phones and tablets, else medium), lowered once if a few seconds of frames are slow (measured once, then remembered).\n63\t- `get debugShown: boolean`: Whether the debug panel is showing.\n64\t- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the debug panel (created the first time it shows, filled at once: the loop fills it only while the game runs).\n65\t- `setQuality(level: QualityLevel): void`\n66\t- `get maxFps: number`: Frame cap of the render loop (0 = display rate).\n67\t- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` without an explicit position start from the view currently on screen. Keeps `?camera=`…\n68\t- `toggleMode(): RenderMode`\n69\t- `toggleResolution(): Resolution`\n70\t- `get availableFilters: readonly string[]`: Every filter id the engine ships (see render/filters.ts).\n71\t- `get filters: readonly string[]`\n72\t- `setFilters(ids: readonly string[]): void`\n73\t- `get look: Look`: The current look (a copy): per-layer pixel art and filters, whole-scene filters.\n74\t- `get lookPresets: Readonly<Record<string, Look>>`: Every named look (LOOK_PRESETS): `engine.setLook(engine.lookPresets.pixel_heroes)`.\n75\t- `setLook(look: Look): void`: Apply a look (render/look.ts); `LOOK_PRESETS` has named ones.\n76\t- `cycleLook(step: 1 | -1): string`: Cycle through LOOK_PRESETS ([ and ] keys).\n77\t- `state(): { version: string; game: string; seed: number; ready: boolean; paused: boolean; time: number; timeScale: number; hitstop: number; screen: ScreenFxState; backend: BackendName; ... 19 more ...; status: string; }`: Snapshot for tests, tooling and agents.\n78\t- `step(n = 1, dt = 1 / 60): void`: Tooling: switch to manual time and advance `n` frames of `dt` seconds (simulation, animation, camera). Nothing is drawn; call `renderer.capture()` to see the result.\n79\t- `loadGame(game: Game, options: { camera?: CameraConfig } = {}): Promise<void>`: Unload the running game and start `game` in the same engine (renderer, canvas, camera rig, input, audio context and debug UI stay).\n80\t- `dispose(): void`: Stop the loop and free everything: game, scene, physics, audio, input listeners, DOM, GPU. With a `loadGame()` in flight, the world and GPU resources are freed once its `setup()` has finished (it never runs against a freed physics world or renderer).\n81\t- `get seed: number`: The seed every level's `ctx.random` comes from (`EngineOptions.seed`, `?seed=`; a replay sets it).\n82\t- `get random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (`ctx.random`).\n83\t- `recording(): Recording | null`: The running level's recording: its seed, when it began and every frame's input since (src/engine/replay.ts). Plain JSON: save it, attach it to a bug, replay it with `replay()`.\n84\t- `replay(rec: Recording, start: () => unknown, o: { frames?: number } = {}): Promise<void>`: Play a recording back: its seed and start time are restored, `start` loads the level it was made in (e.g. `() => engine.loadGame(new MyGame())`; it must not wait on frames, so load without a transition), then every frame runs with the recorded input, in manua…\n85\t=== interface EngineOptions @ 203\n86\t### interface EngineOptions\n87\t\n88\t- `container?: HTMLElement`\n89\t- `resolution?: Resolution`\n90\t- `aspect?: AspectMode`: `adaptive` (default): the art height stays `resolution.height` and the art width follows the screen's aspect (portrait phones fill the screen). `fixed`: always exactly `resolution`, letterboxed. `fill`: adjusts both art dimensions to cover the viewport at an…\n91\t- `maxFps?: number`: Cap the render loop (simulation keeps its fixed 60 Hz physics step). Default 60, so 120 Hz phones don't run the whole pipeline twice per game frame. 0 = display rate.\n92\t- `quality?: QualityOption`: Shadow map quality, or `auto` (default: low on touch devices, else medium; lowered once on a slow start).\n93\t- `loadingScreen?: boolean`: Pixel-styled loading indicator while the renderer, physics and assets load. Default true.\n94\t- `mode?: RenderMode`\n95\t- `edges?: EdgeSettings`\n96\t- `camera?: CameraConfig`: Camera preset + parameters. Pick one per game; not meant to change during play.\n97\t- `filters?: readonly string[]`: Post filters (ids from FILTERS) applied in Pixel mode, in order.\n98\t- `look?: Look`: A full look: pixel art or clean rendering and filters per layer (characters & objects, environment) plus whole-scene filters (render/look.ts). Wins over `filters` and `edges`.\n99\t- `seed?: number`: Seed for `ctx.random`: each level gets its own stream from this and the game's name, so the same seed replays the same game. Default 1. `?seed=` in the URL.\n100\t- `record?: number`: Frames of input the engine keeps for each level (`recording()`). Default `MAX_FRAMES`, an hour at 60 fps (up to some 20 MB of JSON with the mouse moving all the time); 0 records nothing.\n101\t- `debugUI?: boolean`: Debug panel. Default: on in dev (`vite`) or with ?debug=1, off in production builds.\n102\t- `debugKeys?: DebugKeysOption`: Engine hotkeys (P Pixel/Raw, R resolution, ` debug panel, [ ] looks, M mute): developer tools. Default: on with the debug panel (dev, ?debug=1), off in a shipped game, whose players have the same options in the settings. `true` / `false`: always on / off; an…\n103\t- `settings?: { button?: TouchCorner | boolean; key?: string | false }`: The player's settings (`engine.settings`): look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate, sound and debug info, in a panel the ⚙ button opens. `button`: its corner (default, or true: 'top-right'; on a mouse it shows while the pointer moves), or false for n…\n104\t- `touch?: boolean`: On-screen joystick + buttons. Default: auto (coarse pointer), or ?touch=1 / ?touch=0.\n105\t- `touchButtons?: readonly TouchButton[]`: On-screen action buttons. Default: the platformer's (DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS).\n106\t- `touchBar?: boolean`: The touch top bar of the engine hotkeys (ⓘ debug panel, P, R, ◐ looks, ♪ mute), on a touch screen while they are on (see `debugKeys`). Default true; false hides it even then, unless the debug panel is on (`?debug=1`, dev builds).\n107\t- `background?: number`\n108\t\n109\t=== interface Game$ @ 266\n110\t### interface Game\n111\t\n112\tA game is a plain object. The engine owns rendering, physics stepping, camera,\n113\tlighting and debug UI; the game only builds its world and reacts to input.\n114\t\n115\t- `readonly name: string`\n116\t- `readonly args?: unknown`: The plain JSON the game was made with, when its name alone can't rebuild it (a lab's depth and seed, the door a room was entered by): kept in its recordings (`Recording.args`), so a tool can make the same game again to replay one (`npm run replay`, src/games.…\n117\t- `readonly assets?: readonly string[]`: Models `setup` will load (`ctx.loadModel` URLs). Optional: listing them lets the engine start downloading while the renderer and physics initialise.\n118\t- `setup(ctx: GameContext): Promise<void> | void`: Build the level: load models, add colliders, spawn entities.\n119\t- `fixedUpdate?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs before every fixed 60 Hz physics step. Move characters/apply forces here.\n120\t- `ready?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Runs when the engine starts running this game: after `Engine.start` (just before `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`. Install page handles for tools here (`window.__MYGAME__`), so they never exist before the engine can step. An exception is…\n121\t- `update?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs once per rendered frame after physics (animation, pickups, UI state).\n122\t- `cameraTarget(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: World position the camera follows.\n123\t- `eyePosition?(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: First-person eye position (defaults to cameraTarget + 0.7 up).\n124\t- `status?(ctx: GameContext): string`: One-line status for the debug UI (score, lives...).\n125\t- `fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string`: The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives outside physics and `ctx.random` (positions, life, counters, the game's own random streams), so two runs that should match (a replay) can be compared. Only what a replay mu…\n126\t- `snapshot?(ctx: GameContext): unknown`: The game's own state as plain data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): everything a later frame reads that the engine does not keep (its entities, timers, random streams; the handles of its physics bodies). With `restore`, the game has save-states.\n127\t- `restore?(ctx: GameContext, state: unknown): void | Promise<void>`: Put the game back to what `snapshot` returned (`engine.restore()`): the same game, already set up. Find physics bodies again by handle (`ctx.physics.world.getRigidBody(h)`).\n128\t- `beforeRestore?(ctx: GameContext): void | Promise<void>`: Optional: leave whatever mode the game is in before a restore changes anything (a mode with a camera of its own, a dialog, a level): `engine.restore()` calls it first, then checks the snapshot fits (the camera preset) and puts the engine's part back, then `re…\n129\t- `onCameraChange?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called after `engine.setCamera()` swapped the camera preset mid-game. The world, physics and player are untouched; adjust anything that depends on the preset (e.g. side-scroller lane lock, hiding the player model in first person).\n130\t- `dispose?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called when the game is unloaded (`engine.loadGame(other)` or `engine.dispose()`), before the engine removes its scene objects, physics bodies, triggers, particles, HUD and music. Free anything else the game owns: DOM, timers, listeners, HMR hooks.\n131\t\n132\t=== interface GameContext @ 288\n133\t### interface GameContext\n134\t\n135\t- `readonly engine: Engine`\n136\t- `readonly scene: Scene`\n137\t- `readonly physics: Physics`\n138\t- `readonly input: Input`\n139\t- `readonly camera: CameraRig`\n140\t- `readonly loadModel: typeof loadModel`\n141\t- `readonly palette: typeof PALETTE`\n142\t- `readonly time: number`: Game time in seconds since start. Frozen while paused or loading.\n143\t- `readonly random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (src/engine/random.ts): the same engine seed and game give the same numbers. Use it (or forks of it) for everything random; never Math.random.\n144\t- `readonly audio: AudioManager`: Sound effects, music, volumes (src/engine/audio).\n145\t- `readonly particles: Particles`: Pixel particles: `particles.burst('dust', at)` (src/engine/particles).\n146\t- `readonly hud: Hud`: Pixel HUD text and icons in art pixels (src/engine/hud).\n147\t- `readonly lights: LightPool`: Dynamic point lights: `lights.request({ position, color, intensity, radius, flicker })` (src/engine/render/lights.ts). A fixed pool sized by quality, created on first use.\n148\t- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time: `tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' })` (src/engine/tween.ts). Cleared when the level unloads.\n149\t\n150\t=== interface HeadlessOptions @ 305\n151\t### interface HeadlessOptions\n152\t\n153\t`Engine.headless`: the page's options (those about the page do nothing), and where it runs.\n154\t\n155\t- `viewport?: Viewport`: The viewport the art resolution and framing come from, as a page's would (default 960×540 at DPR 1). Pointer aim and HUD layout depend on it: a replay uses its recording's (`Recording.view`).\n156\t- `assets?: AssetReader`: How models and packs are read: `nodeAssets()` (src/engine/node.ts) reads `public/`.\n157\t\n158\t=== class CharacterController @ 1476\n159\t### class CharacterController\n160\t\n161\tKinematic capsule driven by Rapier's KinematicCharacterController, with classic\n162\tplatformer affordances (coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height).\n163\t\n164\tThe body moves in continuous world space; the visual model is interpolated by\n165\tPhysics.bind and only *looks* pixel-aligned through the render pipeline.\n166\t\n167\t- `new CharacterController(physics: Physics, options: CharacterOptions)`\n168\t- `readonly body: RAPIER.RigidBody`\n169\t- `readonly collider: RAPIER.Collider`\n170\t- `readonly controller: RAPIER.KinematicCharacterController`\n171\t- `readonly velocity: Vector3`\n172\t- `grounded: boolean`\n173\t- `facing: number`\n174\t- `get feet: Vector3`: Feet position (bottom of the capsule), continuous world space.\n175\t- `interpolatedFeet(alpha: number): Vector3`: Feet position blended between the previous and current physics step (render use).\n176\t- `setInput(move: Vector3, jumpPressed: boolean, jumpHeld: boolean): void`: Set desired horizontal move direction (world space, length ≤ 1) and jump input.\n177\t- `teleport(position: [number, number, number]): void`\n178\t- `fixedUpdate(dt: number): void`: Call from Physics.update's fixedUpdate callback.\n179\t- `attachAnimations(model: Object3D, clips: readonly AnimationClip[]): void`: Attach a model's animation clips (expects Idle / Walk / Jump).\n180\t- `get animation: CharacterAnim`\n181\t- `updateVisual(model: Object3D, dt: number): void`: Per render frame: pick animation state and advance the mixer.\n182\t\n183\t=== class Terrain @ 4836\n184\t### class Terrain\n185\t\n186\t- `new Terrain(o: TerrainOptions = {})`\n187\t- `readonly nx: number`\n188\t- `readonly nz: number`\n189\t- `readonly sizeX: number`\n190\t- `readonly sizeZ: number`\n191\t- `readonly at: readonly [number, number, number]`\n192\t- `readonly heights: Float32Array`: Heights at the grid points, row by row along z: `heights[ix + iz * (nx + 1)]`.\n193\t- `readonly mesh: Mesh`\n194\t- `collider: RAPIER.Collider | null`\n195\t- `get cell: [number, number]`: Metres between grid points along x and z.\n196\t- `pointX(ix: number): number`: World x, z of grid point (ix, iz).\n197\t- `pointZ(iz: number): number`\n198\t- `generate(f: (x: number, z: number) => number): this`: Fill the heights from `f(x, z)` (world x, z; metres above the terrain's origin), then redraw.\n199\t- `heightAt(x: number, z: number): number`: Height of the surface at world (x, z), in world y: the triangle it is over, as the mesh and the collider have it. Outside the grid: its nearest edge.\n200\t- `normalAt(x: number, z: number, out = new Vector3()): Vector3`: The surface's normal at world (x, z) (central differences over half a cell).\n201\t- `update(): void`: Redraw the mesh from the heights (positions, flat normals, colours per face).\n202\t- `attach(physics: Physics): RAPIER.Collider`: Make (or remake, after `generate` / `erode`) the heightfield collider: a fixed body at the terrain's origin. Returns it.\n203\t- `detach(): void`: Remove the collider (the mesh stays).\n204\t- `dispose(): void`: The collider and the mesh's geometry go (the shared material stays).\n205\t- `erode(o: ErosionOptions = {}): void`: Hydraulic erosion: `droplets` raindrops, each rolling downhill from a random point. A drop carries soil up to a capacity that grows with its speed, water and how steeply it is falling: below capacity it digs (over a small radius), above it, or going uphill, i…\n206\t\n207\t=== class GrassField @ 4300\n208\t### class GrassField\n209\t\n210\t- `new GrassField(o: GrassOptions)`\n211\t- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind along x and z (its length is the strength).\n212\t- `sway: number`: How far blades lean per unit of wind (m at the tip). Default 0.12.\n213\t- `readonly blades: number`: Blades actually placed (the mask can leave fewer than `count`).\n214\t- `push(i: number, at: Vector3Like | null, radius = 0.8): void`: Pusher `i` (0..3): blades within `radius` of `at` part and flatten (radius 0: none).\n215\t- `update(time: number): void`: Per frame (game time).\n216\t- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n217\t\n218\t=== class Vehicle @ 2024\n219\t### class Vehicle\n220\t\n221\t- `new Vehicle(physics: Physics, o: VehicleOptions)`\n222\t- `readonly body: RAPIER.RigidBody`\n223\t- `readonly controller: RAPIER.DynamicRayCastVehicleController`\n224\t- `readonly o: Required<Omit<VehicleOptions, 'at' | 'yaw'>>`\n225\t- `readonly mounts: [number, number, number][]`: Wheels' chassis-space mount points (front left, front right, rear left, rear right).\n226\t- `steering: number`: Smoothed steering (-1..1).\n227\t- `slip: number`: How fast the car slides sideways (m/s), and the angle between where it points and where it goes (radians): drifting when high.\n228\t- `slipAngle: number`\n229\t- `setSuspension(stiffness: number): void`: Spring stiffness of every wheel's suspension (live).\n230\t- `get speed: number`: Speed along the car's heading (m/s, negative reversing). Not Rapier's `currentVehicleSpeed`, which counts the up-and-down too (the suspension holding the body up against gravity every step reads as a crawl when parked).\n231\t- `drive(input: DriveInput, dt: number): void`: One fixed step of driving.\n232\t- `wheelPose(i: number, pos: Vector3, rot: Quaternion, local = false): void`: Where wheel `i` is drawn: on its suspension, turned by steering and spin. In the world, or with `local` in the chassis' own space (a wheel mesh parented to a chassis mesh that a physics binding draws between steps).\n233\t- `get yaw: number`: The chassis' heading (radians about Y; 0 drives toward +Z).\n234\t- `reset(at: readonly [number, number, number], yaw = 0): void`: Put it back on its wheels at `at` (a reset after a roll).\n235\t- `dispose(): void`\n236\t\n237\t=== class NavGrid @ 4486\n238\t### class NavGrid\n239\t\n240\t- `new NavGrid(width: number, height: number, o: NavGridOptions = {})`\n241\t- `static fromRows(rows: readonly string[], o: NavGridOptions & { blocked?: string } = {}): NavGrid`: From an ASCII map: `blocked` characters are closed, everything else open. Default '# ': walls and pits (a ' ' has no floor in RoomKit's maps); past the end of a short row is a wall.\n242\t- `readonly width: number`\n243\t- `readonly height: number`\n244\t- `readonly cell: number`\n245\t- `readonly ox: number`: World x, z of cell (0, 0)'s corner.\n246\t- `readonly oz: number`\n247\t- `readonly open: Uint8Array`: 1 = walkable.\n248\t- `version: number`: Bumped by `setOpen`: flow fields rebuild.\n249\t- `flowBuilds: number`: How many times a flow field was built (tools: should be one per target cell change).\n250\t- `col(x: number): number`\n251\t- `row(z: number): number`\n252\t- `centerX(col: number): number`: World x, z of a cell's centre.\n253\t- `centerZ(row: number): number`\n254\t- `isOpen(col: number, row: number): boolean`\n255\t- `walkable(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is the world point on a walkable cell?\n256\t- `setOpen(col: number, row: number, open: boolean): void`\n257\t- `path(from: readonly [number, number, number], to: readonly [number, number, number]): [number, number, number][] | null`: Shortest path between two world points (x, z used; y kept from `from`): A* over cells, no corner cutting, then smoothed so it only turns where a wall is in the way. Null when there is no way. The first point is `from`, the last `to` (snapped into its cell).\n258\t- `smooth(pts: [number, number, number][]): [number, number, number][]`: Drop every point the path can skip in a straight line.\n259\t- `lineOfSight(ax: number, az: number, bx: number, bz: number, radius = 0): boolean`: Can you see (or walk straight) from a to b? Walks the cells under the segment (a DDA); `radius` > 0 also tests two lines offset sideways (a body that wide fits).\n260\t- `castWall(ax: number, az: number, dx: number, dz: number, max: number): number`: Distance from a along a direction to the first closed cell (at most `max`): the same cell walk as `lineOfSight`, so a vision cone drawn with it shows exactly what a guard can see.\n261\t- `flowTo(x: number, z: number): boolean`: A flow field toward the world point (x, z): the walking distance from every cell to it. Rebuilt only when the target changes cell or the grid changed (`version`). Returns whether it was rebuilt.\n262\t- `flowDistance(x: number, z: number): number`: Walking distance to the flow target from a world point (Infinity: no way).\n263\t- `flowDirection(x: number, z: number, out: [number, number]): boolean`: Which way to go from (x, z) toward the flow target: the unit direction to the lowest-distance neighbour (into `out` [x, z]). Returns false at the target or with no way.\n264\t\n265\t=== class Ragdoll @ 1834\n266\t### class Ragdoll\n267\t\n268\t- `new Ragdoll(physics: Physics, root: Object3D, parts: readonly RagdollPart[], o: RagdollOptions = {})`\n269\t- `readonly parts: readonly RagdollPart[]`\n270\t- `readonly o: Required<RagdollOptions>`\n271\t- `readonly root: Object3D`\n272\t- `get active: boolean`: Whether the bodies exist (a `physics.clear()`, a level unload, removes them: then it is off).\n273\t- `get bodies: RAPIER.RigidBody[]`: The bodies (root part first), while active.\n274\t- `enable(o: { velocity?: Vec3; spin?: Vec3 } = {}): void`: Make the bodies from the model's current pose, moving at `velocity` (and spinning at `spin`, rad/s).\n275\t- `push(at: Vec3 | Vector3, velocity: Vec3): void`: Knock the part nearest `at`: it gains `velocity` (m/s, applied there as an impulse of its own mass, so the same push works whatever the ragdoll's density), and its joints pass some of it on.\n276\t- `mass(): number`: Total mass of the parts (world units), while active.\n277\t- `speed(): number`: Fastest part (m/s): settled when this stays low.\n278\t- `rootPose(out = new Vector3()): { at: Vector3; faceUp: boolean; heading: number }`: Where the root part (the pelvis) is and how it lies: face up (on its back: its forward, +Z, points up) or down, and the heading (yaw) a character lying like that has: on its back its head is behind it (−Z), on its front ahead (+Z). That is the heading to get…\n279\t- `sync(): void`: Write the bodies into the joints (between the last two physics steps). Call every frame after the physics update.\n280\t- `release(o: { ground?: number | ((x: number, z: number) => number); heading?: number; offset?: Vec3 } = {}): { apply(w: number): void }`: Remove the bodies and hand the pose back. The model root moves under the root part (feet at `ground`: a height, or a function of x, z called once the bodies are gone; turned to `heading`, default `rootPose().heading`) without moving the joints in the world; t…\n281\t- `disable(): void`: Remove the bodies (the joints keep the last pose).\n282\t\n283\t=== class HUD\\|class Hud @ 2762\n284\t### class Hud\n285\t\n286\tPixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\n287\tthe post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\n288\tlaid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\n289\t(`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\n290\t\n291\tRetained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\n292\t(clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\n293\tresolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\n294\t\n295\t  ctx.hud.clear();\n296\t  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\n297\t  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\n298\t\n299\t- `new Hud(container: HTMLElement | null)`\n300\t- `canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null`: The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn).\n301\t- `visible: boolean`\n302\t- `get width: number`: HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution).\n303\t- `get height: number`\n304\t- `text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation).\n305\t- `rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor: HudAnchor = 'top-left'): void`: A filled rectangle (panels, bars).\n306\t- `sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\n307\t- `clear(): void`: Remove everything.\n308\t- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\n309\t- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n310\t- `dispose(): void`\n311\t\n312\t=== class Input\\|class InputManager @ 2287\n313\t### class Input\n314\t\n315\tKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n316\t- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n317\t  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n318\t- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n319\t  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n320\t  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n321\t- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n322\t  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n323\t  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n324\t  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n325\t  a frame is not lost.\n326\t- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n327\t\n328\t- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n329\t- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n330\t- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it now. Under pointer lock it is the screen's centre (the crosshair).\n331\t- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n332\t- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n333\t- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n334\t- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n335\t- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n336\t- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n337\t- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n338\t- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n339\t- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n340\t- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n341\t- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everything.\n342\t- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n343\t- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n344\t- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n345\t- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n346\t- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n347\t- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n348\t- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n349\t- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n350\t- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n351\t- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n352\t- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped, while a stale press (made during a pause, a l…\n353\t- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n354\t- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n355\t- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n356\t- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n357\t- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n358\t- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n359\t- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n360\t- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n361\t- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n362\t- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n363\t- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n364\t- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play read…\n365\t- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keep…\n366\t- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n367\t- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n368\t- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n369\t- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n370\t- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n371\t- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n372\t\n373\t=== class Timeline @ 4881\n374\t### class Timeline\n375\t\n376\t- `new Timeline(o: TimelineOptions)`\n377\t- `readonly duration: number`\n378\t- `time: number`: The playhead (s).\n379\t- `playing: boolean`\n380\t- `speed: number`: Playback rate (1: real time, 0.5: slow motion).\n381\t- `ended: boolean`: True once the end has been reached (by playing or skipping).\n382\t- `readonly fired: string[]`: Cue names in the order they fired (tests and tools).\n383\t- `play(): void`\n384\t- `pause(): void`\n385\t- `update(dt: number): void`: Advance by `dt` seconds of game time (times `speed`) while playing, firing the cues passed.\n386\t- `seek(t: number): void`: Move the playhead to `t` without firing the cues in between (scrubbing). Cues before `t` count as done and cues after it will fire again (going back re-arms them). Ignored while skipping (a cue that loops back cannot undo a skip).\n387\t- `skip(): void`: Jump to the end: the cues not yet fired run (except the cosmetic ones), then `onEnd`. Cues after `duration` never fire, played or skipped.\n388\t- `camera(t = this.time): CameraShot | null`: Where the camera is at `t` (default now): on each key at its time, gliding between keys on a smooth curve (Catmull-Rom tangents, so the speed has no jolt at a key; the first and last key of each shot ease in and out). A `cut` key starts a new shot. Null witho…\n389\t- `line(t = this.time): Line | null`: The subtitle showing at `t` (default now), or null.\n390\t- `bars(t = this.time): number`: How far the letterbox bars are in (0..1) at `t` (default now): sliding in at the start, out at the end.\n391\t\n392\t=== class Audio\\|class AudioManager @ 2462\n393\t### class AudioManager\n394\t\n395\tWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\n396\tsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\n397\tpersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n398\t\n399\tBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\n400\tfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n401\t(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\n402\twas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n403\t\n404\t- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n405\t- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n406\t- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n407\t- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n408\t- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n409\t- `context: AudioContext | null`\n410\t- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n411\t- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n412\t- `get muted: boolean`\n413\t- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n414\t- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n415\t- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n416\t- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n417\t- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Audio, the voice keeps its settings silently and starts once audio unlocks.…\n418\t- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n419\t- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n420\t- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n421\t- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n422\t- `stopMusic(): void`\n423\t- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n424\t- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n425\t- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n426\t- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n427\t- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n428\t- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n429\t- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n430\t\n431\t=== class SpriteBatch @ 4780\n432\t### class SpriteBatch\n433\t\n434\t- `new SpriteBatch(sheet: SpriteSheet, o: { capacity: number })`\n435\t- `readonly sheet: SpriteSheet`\n436\t- `spawn(at: readonly [number, number, number], o: SpriteOptions = {}): number`: A new sprite (its index), or -1 when full.\n437\t- `set(i: number, o: SpriteOptions & { at?: readonly [number, number, number] }): void`: Move or change sprite `i`.\n438\t- `put(i: number, x: number, y: number, z: number, frame?: number, flip?: boolean): void`: `set` without the options object, for many sprites every frame: position, then frame and mirror.\n439\t- `at(i: number): [number, number, number]`: Where sprite `i` stands.\n440\t- `frameOf(i: number): number`: The frame sprite `i` shows.\n441\t- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n442\t\n443\t=== class Precipitation @ 4580\n444\t### class Precipitation\n445\t\n446\t- `new Precipitation(o: PrecipitationOptions)`\n447\t- `readonly kind: PrecipitationKind`\n448\t- `readonly area: readonly [number, number, number]`\n449\t- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind (m/s along x and z): rain slants, snow drifts.\n450\t- `get intensity: number`: Share of the drops falling (0..1).\n451\t- `get speed: number`: Fall speed (m/s).\n452\t- `update(dt: number, center: Vector3): void`: Per frame (game time): advance the fall and follow `center`.\n453\t- `splashes(dt: number): number`: How many drops hit the ground in `dt` seconds at this intensity (for splash particles, ripples).\n454\t- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n455\t\n456\t=== class WaterSurface @ 5086\n457\t### class WaterSurface\n458\t\n459\t- `new WaterSurface(o: WaterOptions)`\n460\t- `readonly size: readonly [number, number]`\n461\t- `waves: Wave[]`\n462\t- `readonly ripples: RippleField | null`\n463\t- `readonly uOpacity: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n464\t- `readonly uFoamAt: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n465\t- `setWaves(waves: readonly Wave[]): void`: Change the waves (up to MAX_WAVES; the CPU height follows too).\n466\t- `update(time: number, sun?: Vector3): void`: Per frame: the waves' clock (use physics time: floaters read the same), the ripples, the sun.\n467\t- `heightAt(x: number, z: number, t = this.time): number`: World height of the drawn surface at (x, z) (waves and ripples), at the last `update` time or `t`.\n468\t- `covers(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is (x, z) over this water?\n469\t- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n470\t\n471\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"71ec49de-27ff-45fb-a784-9f3cf302f915","uuid":"57500649-02df-4ec0-a07e-b80543072155","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:27.673Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-build-v2--20261105-162027--x07393c-work/71ec49de-27ff-45fb-a784-9f3cf302f915/tool-results/bmaircpkt.txt","content":"14. three.js and Rapier: `RAPIER`, `THREE`, `TSL`\n15. More: `applySky`, `bayer4`, `BehaviorTree`, `beltMaterial`, `BeltMaterial`, `BoidOptions`, `Boids`, `Branch`, `bt`, `BtNode`, `BtStatus`, `BulletPool`, `CameraKey`, `CameraShake`, `CameraShot`, `ClockState`, `coversPixel`, `createNoise`, `crystalMaterial`, `Cue`, `DECAL_SHAPES`, `DecalOptions`, `Decals`, `DecalShape`, `decodeState`, `drawSheet`, `ease`, `Ease`, `EASE_NAMES`, `EaseName`, `EASES`, `emitter`, `Emitter`, `encodeState`, `ErosionOptions`, `expand`, `FbmOptions`, `FlashOptions`, `forceFieldMaterial`, `GpuSwarm`, `GpuSwarmOptions`, `GrassField`, `GrassOptions`, `groundFog`, `hashString`, `HEADLESS_VIEWPORT`, `HeadlessView`, `HeadlessViewOptions`, `hologramMaterial`, `lavaMaterial`, `Leaf`, `LRule`, `LSystemDef`, `marbleMaterial`, `MAX_FRAMES`, `MAX_PUSHERS`, `MAX_WAVES`, `Mirror`, `MirrorOptions`, `NavGrid`, `NavGridOptions`, `Noise`, `Pattern`, `PatternDef`, `PatternTile`, `patternTiles`, `PLANTS`, `Precipitation`, `PrecipitationKind`, `PrecipitationOptions`, `Recording`, `recordingProblem`, `RenderView`, `RenderViewOptions`, `Rng`, `RopeMesh`, `SCREEN_LAYER`, `ScreenFx`, `ScreenFxState`, `ShaderOptions`, `ShakeState`, `ShockwaveOptions`, `skyAt`, `SkyState`, `Snapshot`, `snapshotProblem`, `SoftMesh`, `SpriteBatch`, `SpriteOptions`, `SpriteSheet`, `swayMaterial`, `swayObject`, `SwayOptions`, `Team`, `Terrain`, `TERRAIN_BANDS`, `TerrainBand`, `TerrainOptions`, `Timeline`, `TimelineLine`, `TimelineOptions`, `TraceRow`, `Trail`, `TrailOptions`, `TRANSITION_CELLS`, `TransitionKind`, `TransitionOptions`, `TRANSITIONS`, `turtle`, `TurtleOptions`, `Tween`, `TweenOptions`, `Tweens`, `View`, `WaterOptions`, `WaterSurface`, `Wfc`, `WfcOptions`, `WfcTile`, `WindUniforms`, `woodMaterial`\n\n=== class Engine @ 132\n### class Engine\n\n- `static readonly version: string`: The engine's version (package.json).\n- `static start(game: Game, options: EngineOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`\n- `static headless(game: Game, options: HeadlessOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`: The engine without a page (docs/engine/headless.md): the same boot, game code and frames as `start`, with a `HeadlessView` that draws nothing, in Node. No DOM, no GPU, no listeners, no AudioContext (`audio.counts` still counts), no panels; errors go to `engin…\n- `readonly errors: string[]`: Errors thrown by the game's hooks while the render loop ran, each message once, oldest first (at most 20). They are logged to the console and shown in a box on screen; the frame keeps rendering so the picture stays up. Fix the first one first. (`step()` throw…\n- `readonly input: Input`\n- `readonly sun: DirectionalLight`\n- `readonly ambient: AmbientLight`\n- `readonly context: GameContext`\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`\n- `readonly particles: Particles`\n- `readonly hud: Hud`\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time (`ctx.tweens`).\n- `readonly shake: CameraShake`: Screen shake (trauma 0..1): `engine.shake.add(0.4)`. Added to the camera after the rig placed it, on real time; ortho cameras move by whole art pixels. Reset on level unload.\n- `debug: DebugUI | null`\n- `touch: TouchControls | null`\n- `readonly settings: Settings`: The player's settings: look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate and debug info (plain data, saved per game), applied over the game's own choices; `open()` shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button, `EngineOptions.settings`). Sound is `audio`'s.\n- `debugKeys: DebugKeyMap`: Active engine hotkeys (from EngineOptions.debugKeys; none until the engine starts).\n- `manual: boolean`: Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`. Frame-exact and independent of how fast the browser renders (filmstrips, replays).\n- `frame: number`\n- `fps: number`: Rendered frames per second (measured over ~0.5 s).\n- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`: Requested quality (`auto` adapts); `quality` is the level in use.\n- `time: number`\n- `readonly renderer: View`\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\n- `camera: CameraRig`\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\n- `get gpu: PixelRenderer | null`: The page's renderer, for direct GPU work (readbacks, the canvas, compute): null headless (`Engine.headless`), where nothing is drawn. Gameplay reads `renderer`, which both have.\n- `get game: Game`: The running game. Replace it with `loadGame()`.\n- `get paused: boolean`: Freeze simulation, animation, particles and game time (rendering continues). For tooling, capture and pause menus. Queued key presses are dropped while paused.\n- `get lights: LightPool`: The dynamic light pool (render/lights.ts), engine-owned: its lights stay in the scene across levels, so loading another level never changes the light count (no recompiles). Created on first use; sized by quality.\n- `get timeScale: number`: Game speed: 1 normal, 0.25 slow motion, 2 double, 0 frozen (a menu: key presses made meanwhile are dropped, like a pause). Scales game time, physics, animation, particles, tweens and the camera follow; not transitions, shake or the render loop. Back to 1 when…\n- `hitstop(seconds: number): void`: Freeze the game for `seconds` of real time: impact frames that sell a hit. Rendering, screen shake and transitions keep going; overlapping hitstops keep the longest. Key presses made during a hitstop still count afterwards (a buffered jump).\n- `get screen: ScreenFx`: Transitions, flashes and shockwaves (render/screenFx.ts): `await engine.screen.cover('iris')`.\n- `get sunDir: Vector3`: The direction sunlight comes from (unit, pointing at the sun).\n- `setSunDirection(dir: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]): void`: Point the sun (and its shadows): a direction toward the sun, normalised here. A level's time of day or a sun dial; reset to the engine default when the level unloads. Below the horizon the sun is kept just above it (lower `sun.intensity` for night instead).\n- `get quality: QualityLevel`: The quality level in use (see `EngineOptions.quality`).\n- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`: Quality by option: a level, or `auto`: the device's level (low on phones and tablets, else medium), lowered once if a few seconds of frames are slow (measured once, then remembered).\n- `get debugShown: boolean`: Whether the debug panel is showing.\n- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the debug panel (created the first time it shows, filled at once: the loop fills it only while the game runs).\n- `setQuality(level: QualityLevel): void`\n- `get maxFps: number`: Frame cap of the render loop (0 = display rate).\n- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` without an explicit position start from the view currently on screen. Keeps `?camera=`…\n- `toggleMode(): RenderMode`\n- `toggleResolution(): Resolution`\n- `get availableFilters: readonly string[]`: Every filter id the engine ships (see render/filters.ts).\n- `get filters: readonly string[]`\n- `setFilters(ids: readonly string[]): void`\n- `get look: Look`: The current look (a copy): per-layer pixel art and filters, whole-scene filters.\n- `get lookPresets: Readonly<Record<string, Look>>`: Every named look (LOOK_PRESETS): `engine.setLook(engine.lookPresets.pixel_heroes)`.\n- `setLook(look: Look): void`: Apply a look (render/look.ts); `LOOK_PRESETS` has named ones.\n- `cycleLook(step: 1 | -1): string`: Cycle through LOOK_PRESETS ([ and ] keys).\n- `state(): { version: string; game: string; seed: number; ready: boolean; paused: boolean; time: number; timeScale: number; hitstop: number; screen: ScreenFxState; backend: BackendName; ... 19 more ...; status: string; }`: Snapshot for tests, tooling and agents.\n- `step(n = 1, dt = 1 / 60): void`: Tooling: switch to manual time and advance `n` frames of `dt` seconds (simulation, animation, camera). Nothing is drawn; call `renderer.capture()` to see the result.\n- `loadGame(game: Game, options: { camera?: CameraConfig } = {}): Promise<void>`: Unload the running game and start `game` in the same engine (renderer, canvas, camera rig, input, audio context and debug UI stay).\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop the loop and free everything: game, scene, physics, audio, input listeners, DOM, GPU. With a `loadGame()` in flight, the world and GPU resources are freed once its `setup()` has finished (it never runs against a freed physics world or renderer).\n- `get seed: number`: The seed every level's `ctx.random` comes from (`EngineOptions.seed`, `?seed=`; a replay sets it).\n- `get random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (`ctx.random`).\n- `recording(): Recording | null`: The running level's recording: its seed, when it began and every frame's input since (src/engine/replay.ts). Plain JSON: save it, attach it to a bug, replay it with `replay()`.\n- `replay(rec: Recording, start: () => unknown, o: { frames?: number } = {}): Promise<void>`: Play a recording back: its seed and start time are restored, `start` loads the level it was made in (e.g. `() => engine.loadGame(new MyGame())`; it must not wait on frames, so load without a transition), then every frame runs with the recorded input, in manua…\n=== interface EngineOptions @ 203\n### interface EngineOptions\n\n- `container?: HTMLElement`\n- `resolution?: Resolution`\n- `aspect?: AspectMode`: `adaptive` (default): the art height stays `resolution.height` and the art width follows the screen's aspect (portrait phones fill the screen). `fixed`: always exactly `resolution`, letterboxed. `fill`: adjusts both art dimensions to cover the viewport at an…\n- `maxFps?: number`: Cap the render loop (simulation keeps its fixed 60 Hz physics step). Default 60, so 120 Hz phones don't run the whole pipeline twice per game frame. 0 = display rate.\n- `quality?: QualityOption`: Shadow map quality, or `auto` (default: low on touch devices, else medium; lowered once on a slow start).\n- `loadingScreen?: boolean`: Pixel-styled loading indicator while the renderer, physics and assets load. Default true.\n- `mode?: RenderMode`\n- `edges?: EdgeSettings`\n- `camera?: CameraConfig`: Camera preset + parameters. Pick one per game; not meant to change during play.\n- `filters?: readonly string[]`: Post filters (ids from FILTERS) applied in Pixel mode, in order.\n- `look?: Look`: A full look: pixel art or clean rendering and filters per layer (characters & objects, environment) plus whole-scene filters (render/look.ts). Wins over `filters` and `edges`.\n- `seed?: number`: Seed for `ctx.random`: each level gets its own stream from this and the game's name, so the same seed replays the same game. Default 1. `?seed=` in the URL.\n- `record?: number`: Frames of input the engine keeps for each level (`recording()`). Default `MAX_FRAMES`, an hour at 60 fps (up to some 20 MB of JSON with the mouse moving all the time); 0 records nothing.\n- `debugUI?: boolean`: Debug panel. Default: on in dev (`vite`) or with ?debug=1, off in production builds.\n- `debugKeys?: DebugKeysOption`: Engine hotkeys (P Pixel/Raw, R resolution, ` debug panel, [ ] looks, M mute): developer tools. Default: on with the debug panel (dev, ?debug=1), off in a shipped game, whose players have the same options in the settings. `true` / `false`: always on / off; an…\n- `settings?: { button?: TouchCorner | boolean; key?: string | false }`: The player's settings (`engine.settings`): look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate, sound and debug info, in a panel the ⚙ button opens. `button`: its corner (default, or true: 'top-right'; on a mouse it shows while the pointer moves), or false for n…\n- `touch?: boolean`: On-screen joystick + buttons. Default: auto (coarse pointer), or ?touch=1 / ?touch=0.\n- `touchButtons?: readonly TouchButton[]`: On-screen action buttons. Default: the platformer's (DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS).\n- `touchBar?: boolean`: The touch top bar of the engine hotkeys (ⓘ debug panel, P, R, ◐ looks, ♪ mute), on a touch screen while they are on (see `debugKeys`). Default true; false hides it even then, unless the debug panel is on (`?debug=1`, dev builds).\n- `background?: number`\n\n=== interface Game$ @ 266\n### interface Game\n\nA game is a plain object. The engine owns rendering, physics stepping, camera,\nlighting and debug UI; the game only builds its world and reacts to input.\n\n- `readonly name: string`\n- `readonly args?: unknown`: The plain JSON the game was made with, when its name alone can't rebuild it (a lab's depth and seed, the door a room was entered by): kept in its recordings (`Recording.args`), so a tool can make the same game again to replay one (`npm run replay`, src/games.…\n- `readonly assets?: readonly string[]`: Models `setup` will load (`ctx.loadModel` URLs). Optional: listing them lets the engine start downloading while the renderer and physics initialise.\n- `setup(ctx: GameContext): Promise<void> | void`: Build the level: load models, add colliders, spawn entities.\n- `fixedUpdate?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs before every fixed 60 Hz physics step. Move characters/apply forces here.\n- `ready?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Runs when the engine starts running this game: after `Engine.start` (just before `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`. Install page handles for tools here (`window.__MYGAME__`), so they never exist before the engine can step. An exception is…\n- `update?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void`: Runs once per rendered frame after physics (animation, pickups, UI state).\n- `cameraTarget(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: World position the camera follows.\n- `eyePosition?(ctx: GameContext): Vector3`: First-person eye position (defaults to cameraTarget + 0.7 up).\n- `status?(ctx: GameContext): string`: One-line status for the debug UI (score, lives...).\n- `fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string`: The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives outside physics and `ctx.random` (positions, life, counters, the game's own random streams), so two runs that should match (a replay) can be compared. Only what a replay mu…\n- `snapshot?(ctx: GameContext): unknown`: The game's own state as plain data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): everything a later frame reads that the engine does not keep (its entities, timers, random streams; the handles of its physics bodies). With `restore`, the game has save-states.\n- `restore?(ctx: GameContext, state: unknown): void | Promise<void>`: Put the game back to what `snapshot` returned (`engine.restore()`): the same game, already set up. Find physics bodies again by handle (`ctx.physics.world.getRigidBody(h)`).\n- `beforeRestore?(ctx: GameContext): void | Promise<void>`: Optional: leave whatever mode the game is in before a restore changes anything (a mode with a camera of its own, a dialog, a level): `engine.restore()` calls it first, then checks the snapshot fits (the camera preset) and puts the engine's part back, then `re…\n- `onCameraChange?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called after `engine.setCamera()` swapped the camera preset mid-game. The world, physics and player are untouched; adjust anything that depends on the preset (e.g. side-scroller lane lock, hiding the player model in first person).\n- `dispose?(ctx: GameContext): void`: Called when the game is unloaded (`engine.loadGame(other)` or `engine.dispose()`), before the engine removes its scene objects, physics bodies, triggers, particles, HUD and music. Free anything else the game owns: DOM, timers, listeners, HMR hooks.\n\n=== interface GameContext @ 288\n### interface GameContext\n\n- `readonly engine: Engine`\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\n- `readonly input: Input`\n- `readonly camera: CameraRig`\n- `readonly loadModel: typeof loadModel`\n- `readonly palette: typeof PALETTE`\n- `readonly time: number`: Game time in seconds since start. Frozen while paused or loading.\n- `readonly random: Rng`: This level's seeded randomness (src/engine/random.ts): the same engine seed and game give the same numbers. Use it (or forks of it) for everything random; never Math.random.\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`: Sound effects, music, volumes (src/engine/audio).\n- `readonly particles: Particles`: Pixel particles: `particles.burst('dust', at)` (src/engine/particles).\n- `readonly hud: Hud`: Pixel HUD text and icons in art pixels (src/engine/hud).\n- `readonly lights: LightPool`: Dynamic point lights: `lights.request({ position, color, intensity, radius, flicker })` (src/engine/render/lights.ts). A fixed pool sized by quality, created on first use.\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time: `tweens.to(door.position, { y: 3 }, { duration: 0.8, ease: 'outBack' })` (src/engine/tween.ts). Cleared when the level unloads.\n\n=== interface HeadlessOptions @ 305\n### interface HeadlessOptions\n\n`Engine.headless`: the page's options (those about the page do nothing), and where it runs.\n\n- `viewport?: Viewport`: The viewport the art resolution and framing come from, as a page's would (default 960×540 at DPR 1). Pointer aim and HUD layout depend on it: a replay uses its recording's (`Recording.view`).\n- `assets?: AssetReader`: How models and packs are read: `nodeAssets()` (src/engine/node.ts) reads `public/`.\n\n=== class CharacterController @ 1476\n### class CharacterController\n\nKinematic capsule driven by Rapier's KinematicCharacterController, with classic\nplatformer affordances (coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height).\n\nThe body moves in continuous world space; the visual model is interpolated by\nPhysics.bind and only *looks* pixel-aligned through the render pipeline.\n\n- `new CharacterController(physics: Physics, options: CharacterOptions)`\n- `readonly body: RAPIER.RigidBody`\n- `readonly collider: RAPIER.Collider`\n- `readonly controller: RAPIER.KinematicCharacterController`\n- `readonly velocity: Vector3`\n- `grounded: boolean`\n- `facing: number`\n- `get feet: Vector3`: Feet position (bottom of the capsule), continuous world space.\n- `interpolatedFeet(alpha: number): Vector3`: Feet position blended between the previous and current physics step (render use).\n- `setInput(move: Vector3, jumpPressed: boolean, jumpHeld: boolean): void`: Set desired horizontal move direction (world space, length ≤ 1) and jump input.\n- `teleport(position: [number, number, number]): void`\n- `fixedUpdate(dt: number): void`: Call from Physics.update's fixedUpdate callback.\n- `attachAnimations(model: Object3D, clips: readonly AnimationClip[]): void`: Attach a model's animation clips (expects Idle / Walk / Jump).\n- `get animation: CharacterAnim`\n- `updateVisual(model: Object3D, dt: number): void`: Per render frame: pick animation state and advance the mixer.\n\n=== class Terrain @ 4836\n### class Terrain\n\n- `new Terrain(o: TerrainOptions = {})`\n- `readonly nx: number`\n- `readonly nz: number`\n- `readonly sizeX: number`\n- `readonly sizeZ: number`\n- `readonly at: readonly [number, number, number]`\n- `readonly heights: Float32Array`: Heights at the grid points, row by row along z: `heights[ix + iz * (nx + 1)]`.\n- `readonly mesh: Mesh`\n- `collider: RAPIER.Collider | null`\n- `get cell: [number, number]`: Metres between grid points along x and z.\n- `pointX(ix: number): number`: World x, z of grid point (ix, iz).\n- `pointZ(iz: number): number`\n- `generate(f: (x: number, z: number) => number): this`: Fill the heights from `f(x, z)` (world x, z; metres above the terrain's origin), then redraw.\n- `heightAt(x: number, z: number): number`: Height of the surface at world (x, z), in world y: the triangle it is over, as the mesh and the collider have it. Outside the grid: its nearest edge.\n- `normalAt(x: number, z: number, out = new Vector3()): Vector3`: The surface's normal at world (x, z) (central differences over half a cell).\n- `update(): void`: Redraw the mesh from the heights (positions, flat normals, colours per face).\n- `attach(physics: Physics): RAPIER.Collider`: Make (or remake, after `generate` / `erode`) the heightfield collider: a fixed body at the terrain's origin. Returns it.\n- `detach(): void`: Remove the collider (the mesh stays).\n- `dispose(): void`: The collider and the mesh's geometry go (the shared material stays).\n- `erode(o: ErosionOptions = {}): void`: Hydraulic erosion: `droplets` raindrops, each rolling downhill from a random point. A drop carries soil up to a capacity that grows with its speed, water and how steeply it is falling: below capacity it digs (over a small radius), above it, or going uphill, i…\n\n=== class GrassField @ 4300\n### class GrassField\n\n- `new GrassField(o: GrassOptions)`\n- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind along x and z (its length is the strength).\n- `sway: number`: How far blades lean per unit of wind (m at the tip). Default 0.12.\n- `readonly blades: number`: Blades actually placed (the mask can leave fewer than `count`).\n- `push(i: number, at: Vector3Like | null, radius = 0.8): void`: Pusher `i` (0..3): blades within `radius` of `at` part and flatten (radius 0: none).\n- `update(time: number): void`: Per frame (game time).\n- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n\n=== class Vehicle @ 2024\n### class Vehicle\n\n- `new Vehicle(physics: Physics, o: VehicleOptions)`\n- `readonly body: RAPIER.RigidBody`\n- `readonly controller: RAPIER.DynamicRayCastVehicleController`\n- `readonly o: Required<Omit<VehicleOptions, 'at' | 'yaw'>>`\n- `readonly mounts: [number, number, number][]`: Wheels' chassis-space mount points (front left, front right, rear left, rear right).\n- `steering: number`: Smoothed steering (-1..1).\n- `slip: number`: How fast the car slides sideways (m/s), and the angle between where it points and where it goes (radians): drifting when high.\n- `slipAngle: number`\n- `setSuspension(stiffness: number): void`: Spring stiffness of every wheel's suspension (live).\n- `get speed: number`: Speed along the car's heading (m/s, negative reversing). Not Rapier's `currentVehicleSpeed`, which counts the up-and-down too (the suspension holding the body up against gravity every step reads as a crawl when parked).\n- `drive(input: DriveInput, dt: number): void`: One fixed step of driving.\n- `wheelPose(i: number, pos: Vector3, rot: Quaternion, local = false): void`: Where wheel `i` is drawn: on its suspension, turned by steering and spin. In the world, or with `local` in the chassis' own space (a wheel mesh parented to a chassis mesh that a physics binding draws between steps).\n- `get yaw: number`: The chassis' heading (radians about Y; 0 drives toward +Z).\n- `reset(at: readonly [number, number, number], yaw = 0): void`: Put it back on its wheels at `at` (a reset after a roll).\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n=== class NavGrid @ 4486\n### class NavGrid\n\n- `new NavGrid(width: number, height: number, o: NavGridOptions = {})`\n- `static fromRows(rows: readonly string[], o: NavGridOptions & { blocked?: string } = {}): NavGrid`: From an ASCII map: `blocked` characters are closed, everything else open. Default '# ': walls and pits (a ' ' has no floor in RoomKit's maps); past the end of a short row is a wall.\n- `readonly width: number`\n- `readonly height: number`\n- `readonly cell: number`\n- `readonly ox: number`: World x, z of cell (0, 0)'s corner.\n- `readonly oz: number`\n- `readonly open: Uint8Array`: 1 = walkable.\n- `version: number`: Bumped by `setOpen`: flow fields rebuild.\n- `flowBuilds: number`: How many times a flow field was built (tools: should be one per target cell change).\n- `col(x: number): number`\n- `row(z: number): number`\n- `centerX(col: number): number`: World x, z of a cell's centre.\n- `centerZ(row: number): number`\n- `isOpen(col: number, row: number): boolean`\n- `walkable(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is the world point on a walkable cell?\n- `setOpen(col: number, row: number, open: boolean): void`\n- `path(from: readonly [number, number, number], to: readonly [number, number, number]): [number, number, number][] | null`: Shortest path between two world points (x, z used; y kept from `from`): A* over cells, no corner cutting, then smoothed so it only turns where a wall is in the way. Null when there is no way. The first point is `from`, the last `to` (snapped into its cell).\n- `smooth(pts: [number, number, number][]): [number, number, number][]`: Drop every point the path can skip in a straight line.\n- `lineOfSight(ax: number, az: number, bx: number, bz: number, radius = 0): boolean`: Can you see (or walk straight) from a to b? Walks the cells under the segment (a DDA); `radius` > 0 also tests two lines offset sideways (a body that wide fits).\n- `castWall(ax: number, az: number, dx: number, dz: number, max: number): number`: Distance from a along a direction to the first closed cell (at most `max`): the same cell walk as `lineOfSight`, so a vision cone drawn with it shows exactly what a guard can see.\n- `flowTo(x: number, z: number): boolean`: A flow field toward the world point (x, z): the walking distance from every cell to it. Rebuilt only when the target changes cell or the grid changed (`version`). Returns whether it was rebuilt.\n- `flowDistance(x: number, z: number): number`: Walking distance to the flow target from a world point (Infinity: no way).\n- `flowDirection(x: number, z: number, out: [number, number]): boolean`: Which way to go from (x, z) toward the flow target: the unit direction to the lowest-distance neighbour (into `out` [x, z]). Returns false at the target or with no way.\n\n=== class Ragdoll @ 1834\n### class Ragdoll\n\n- `new Ragdoll(physics: Physics, root: Object3D, parts: readonly RagdollPart[], o: RagdollOptions = {})`\n- `readonly parts: readonly RagdollPart[]`\n- `readonly o: Required<RagdollOptions>`\n- `readonly root: Object3D`\n- `get active: boolean`: Whether the bodies exist (a `physics.clear()`, a level unload, removes them: then it is off).\n- `get bodies: RAPIER.RigidBody[]`: The bodies (root part first), while active.\n- `enable(o: { velocity?: Vec3; spin?: Vec3 } = {}): void`: Make the bodies from the model's current pose, moving at `velocity` (and spinning at `spin`, rad/s).\n- `push(at: Vec3 | Vector3, velocity: Vec3): void`: Knock the part nearest `at`: it gains `velocity` (m/s, applied there as an impulse of its own mass, so the same push works whatever the ragdoll's density), and its joints pass some of it on.\n- `mass(): number`: Total mass of the parts (world units), while active.\n- `speed(): number`: Fastest part (m/s): settled when this stays low.\n- `rootPose(out = new Vector3()): { at: Vector3; faceUp: boolean; heading: number }`: Where the root part (the pelvis) is and how it lies: face up (on its back: its forward, +Z, points up) or down, and the heading (yaw) a character lying like that has: on its back its head is behind it (−Z), on its front ahead (+Z). That is the heading to get…\n- `sync(): void`: Write the bodies into the joints (between the last two physics steps). Call every frame after the physics update.\n- `release(o: { ground?: number | ((x: number, z: number) => number); heading?: number; offset?: Vec3 } = {}): { apply(w: number): void }`: Remove the bodies and hand the pose back. The model root moves under the root part (feet at `ground`: a height, or a function of x, z called once the bodies are gone; turned to `heading`, default `rootPose().heading`) without moving the joints in the world; t…\n- `disable(): void`: Remove the bodies (the joints keep the last pose).\n\n=== class HUD\\|class Hud @ 2762\n### class Hud\n\nPixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\nthe post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\nlaid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\n(`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\n\nRetained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\n(clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\nresolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\n\n  ctx.hud.clear();\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\n\n- `new Hud(container: HTMLElement | null)`\n- `canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null`: The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn).\n- `visible: boolean`\n- `get width: number`: HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution).\n- `get height: number`\n- `text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation).\n- `rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor: HudAnchor = 'top-left'): void`: A filled rectangle (panels, bars).\n- `sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\n- `clear(): void`: Remove everything.\n- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\n- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n=== class Input\\|class InputManager @ 2287\n### class Input\n\nKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n  a frame is not lost.\n- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n\n- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it now. Under pointer lock it is the screen's centre (the crosshair).\n- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everything.\n- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped, while a stale press (made during a pause, a l…\n- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n- `get buttonEvents: readonly ButtonEvent[]`: This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play read…\n- `send(command: unknown): void`: Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked, a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keep…\n- `get commands: readonly unknown[]`: This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\n- `deliver(): void`: Engine: a live frame begins to run: what was sent since the last one becomes its `commands`.\n- `addPointer(dx: number, dy: number, wheel = 0): void`: Test hook: inject pointer movement / wheel for the next frame.\n- `snapshot(dt: number, last?: SnapshotMemory): InputFrame`: This frame's input as data (after `beginFrame`), for a recording. `last` is the frame before: unchanged held keys, pointer and gamepad connection are left out.\n- `playBefore(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, before the engine's `beginFrame`: the frame's held keys and presses (presses queue at the time they would have arrived, the frame before's game time).\n- `playAfter(f: InputFrame): void`: Replay, after `beginFrame` (which polled no gamepads): sticks, pointer, wheel, gamepad connection.\n\n=== class Timeline @ 4881\n### class Timeline\n\n- `new Timeline(o: TimelineOptions)`\n- `readonly duration: number`\n- `time: number`: The playhead (s).\n- `playing: boolean`\n- `speed: number`: Playback rate (1: real time, 0.5: slow motion).\n- `ended: boolean`: True once the end has been reached (by playing or skipping).\n- `readonly fired: string[]`: Cue names in the order they fired (tests and tools).\n- `play(): void`\n- `pause(): void`\n- `update(dt: number): void`: Advance by `dt` seconds of game time (times `speed`) while playing, firing the cues passed.\n- `seek(t: number): void`: Move the playhead to `t` without firing the cues in between (scrubbing). Cues before `t` count as done and cues after it will fire again (going back re-arms them). Ignored while skipping (a cue that loops back cannot undo a skip).\n- `skip(): void`: Jump to the end: the cues not yet fired run (except the cosmetic ones), then `onEnd`. Cues after `duration` never fire, played or skipped.\n- `camera(t = this.time): CameraShot | null`: Where the camera is at `t` (default now): on each key at its time, gliding between keys on a smooth curve (Catmull-Rom tangents, so the speed has no jolt at a key; the first and last key of each shot ease in and out). A `cut` key starts a new shot. Null witho…\n- `line(t = this.time): Line | null`: The subtitle showing at `t` (default now), or null.\n- `bars(t = this.time): number`: How far the letterbox bars are in (0..1) at `t` (default now): sliding in at the start, out at the end.\n\n=== class Audio\\|class AudioManager @ 2462\n### class AudioManager\n\nWeb Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\nsequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\npersisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\n\nBrowsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\nfirst key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\n(headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\nwas played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\n\n- `new AudioManager(target: EventTarget | null = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : null)`\n- `static get supported: boolean`: Web Audio exists in this environment.\n- `readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>`: Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded.\n- `readonly log: { name: string; at: number }[]`: The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests.\n- `readonly counts: Record<string, number>`: How many times each sound was requested.\n- `context: AudioContext | null`\n- `get unlocked: boolean`: True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input).\n- `get volume: Readonly<AudioSettings>`: Current volumes and mute (persisted).\n- `get muted: boolean`\n- `get playing: string`: Name of the playing song ('' when none).\n- `unlock(): void`: Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\n- `register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void`: Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef).\n- `play(sound: string | SoundDef, options: PlayOptions = {}): boolean`: Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\n- `loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings: VoiceSettings = {}): Voice`: Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch: with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with no Web Audio, the voice keeps its settings silently and starts once audio unlocks.…\n- `get loops: readonly Voice[]`: Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests).\n- `stopLoops(): void`: Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads).\n- `load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>`: Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\n- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n- `stopMusic(): void`\n- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n\n=== class SpriteBatch @ 4780\n### class SpriteBatch\n\n- `new SpriteBatch(sheet: SpriteSheet, o: { capacity: number })`\n- `readonly sheet: SpriteSheet`\n- `spawn(at: readonly [number, number, number], o: SpriteOptions = {}): number`: A new sprite (its index), or -1 when full.\n- `set(i: number, o: SpriteOptions & { at?: readonly [number, number, number] }): void`: Move or change sprite `i`.\n- `put(i: number, x: number, y: number, z: number, frame?: number, flip?: boolean): void`: `set` without the options object, for many sprites every frame: position, then frame and mirror.\n- `at(i: number): [number, number, number]`: Where sprite `i` stands.\n- `frameOf(i: number): number`: The frame sprite `i` shows.\n- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n\n=== class Precipitation @ 4580\n### class Precipitation\n\n- `new Precipitation(o: PrecipitationOptions)`\n- `readonly kind: PrecipitationKind`\n- `readonly area: readonly [number, number, number]`\n- `readonly wind: Vector2`: Wind (m/s along x and z): rain slants, snow drifts.\n- `get intensity: number`: Share of the drops falling (0..1).\n- `get speed: number`: Fall speed (m/s).\n- `update(dt: number, center: Vector3): void`: Per frame (game time): advance the fall and follow `center`.\n- `splashes(dt: number): number`: How many drops hit the ground in `dt` seconds at this intensity (for splash particles, ripples).\n- `dispose(): this`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance\n\n=== class WaterSurface @ 5086\n### class WaterSurface\n\n- `new WaterSurface(o: WaterOptions)`\n- `readonly size: readonly [number, number]`\n- `waves: Wave[]`\n- `readonly ripples: RippleField | null`\n- `readonly uOpacity: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n- `readonly uFoamAt: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`\n- `setWaves(waves: readonly Wave[]): void`: Change the waves (up to MAX_WAVES; the CPU height follows too).\n- `update(time: number, sun?: Vector3): void`: Per frame: the waves' clock (use physics time: floaters read the same), the ripples, the sun.\n- `heightAt(x: number, z: number, t = this.time): number`: World height of the drawn surface at (x, z) (waves and ripples), at the last `update` time or `t`.\n- `covers(x: number, z: number): boolean`: Is (x, z) over this water?\n- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n\n","numLines":452,"startLine":20,"totalLines":471}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01LNXti6ae7ib6cBcX9hC2FR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
