{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CNSL8tPyf9NcLQqQiFpNRD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t### interface TerrainOptions\n2\t\n3\t- `size?: readonly [number, number]`: Size in metres along x and z (default [32, 32]).\n4\t- `cells?: readonly [number, number]`: Cells along x and z (default one per metre).\n5\t- `at?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Where the middle of the grid's height 0 is (default the origin).\n6\t- `bands?: readonly TerrainBand[]`: Colour by height, lowest first; the last band takes everything above.\n7\t- `steep?: number`: Faces steeper than this (their normal's y below it) are rock. Default 0.72 (about 44°).\n8\t- `rock?: number`\n9\t\n10\t### interface GrassOptions\n11\t\n12\t- `area: readonly [number, number]`: Width (x) and depth (z) of the patch, m.\n13\t- `at: readonly [number, number, number]`: Centre of the patch on the ground.\n14\t- `count: number`\n15\t- `height?: readonly [number, number]`: Blade heights (m): shortest and tallest. Default [0.25, 0.55].\n16\t- `width?: number`: Blade width at the root (m). Default 0.07.\n17\t- `base?: number`: Colours (hex): roots and tips.\n18\t- `tip?: number`\n19\t- `mask?: (x: number, z: number) => boolean`: Where blades grow (world x, z); default everywhere in the patch.\n20\t- `ground?: (x: number, z: number) => number`: Ground height under a blade (default: the patch's y).\n21\t- `seed?: number`\n22\t\n23\t### interface CharacterOptions\n24\t\n25\t- `position: [number, number, number]`\n26\t- `radius?: number`\n27\t- `halfHeight?: number`: Height of the cylindrical part of the capsule.\n28\t- `speed?: number`\n29\t- `jumpSpeed?: number`\n30\t- `gravity?: number`\n31\t\n32\t### interface VehicleOptions\n33\t\n34\t- `at: readonly [number, number, number]`\n35\t- `yaw?: number`: Heading (radians about Y; 0 drives toward +Z).\n36\t- `size?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Chassis half extents (m). Default [0.9, 0.35, 1.8].\n37\t- `mass?: number`: Chassis mass in world units. Default 8.\n38\t- `wheelRadius?: number`\n39\t- `suspension?: number`: Suspension rest length and stiffness.\n40\t- `stiffness?: number`\n41\t- `power?: number`: Forward drive force per driven wheel at full throttle. Default 30.\n42\t- `topSpeed?: number`: The engine gives out toward this speed (m/s). Default 16.\n43\t- `steer?: number`: Most steering angle (radians). Default 0.55.\n44\t- `grip?: number`: Grip (friction slip): how much sideways force the tyres hold before sliding. Default 2.2.\n45\t- `driftYaw?: number`: Most spin (rad/s) the handbrake's drift push builds up. Default 2.4; 0 turns the assist off.\n46\t\n47\t### interface DriveInput\n48\t\n49\t- `throttle: number`: -1 (reverse) … 1\n50\t- `steer: number`: -1 (left) … 1 (right)\n51\t- `brake?: boolean`\n52\t- `handbrake?: boolean`\n53\t\n54\t### interface SoundDef\n55\t\n56\t- `wave: Wave`\n57\t- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n58\t- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n59\t- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n60\t- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n61\t- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n62\t- `sustain?: number`\n63\t- `decay?: number`\n64\t- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n65\t- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n66\t- `arpRate?: number`\n67\t- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n68\t- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n69\t- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n70\t- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n71\t\n72\t### interface Song\n73\t\n74\tA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\n75\twhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\n76\tEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\n77\tnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n78\t\n79\t- `bpm: number`\n80\t- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n81\t- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n82\t- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n83\t- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n84\t- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n85\t\n86\t### interface VoiceSettings\n87\t\n88\t- `volume?: number`: 0..1 (default 1).\n89\t- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n90\t- `muffle?: number`: 0 (clear) .. 1 (behind a thick wall, under water): a low-pass filter closing down.\n91\t- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones.\n92\t\n93\t### interface PlayOptions\n94\t\n95\t- `volume?: number`: 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume.\n96\t- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones (playback rate), e.g. a rising combo.\n97\t- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n98\t\n99\t### interface HudOptions\n100\t\n101\t- `color?: HudColor`: Default 'white'.\n102\t- `shadow?: HudColor | false`: 1-pixel drop shadow color, or false. Default 'ink'.\n103\t- `scale?: number`: Integer art-pixel scale (2 = double-size glyphs). Default 1.\n104\t- `anchor?: HudAnchor`: Default 'top-left': x/y are offsets from that screen corner/edge, in art pixels.\n105\t\n106\t### class Physics\n107\t\n108\tRapier world with a fixed 60 Hz step and render interpolation.\n109\t\n110\tPhysics owns the truth; visuals follow. Bound Object3Ds are *interpolated* between\n111\tthe last two physics states for smooth motion. Pixel alignment is purely a camera /\n112\tpresentation concern and never touches bodies here.\n113\t\n114\t- `static create(gravity = -24): Promise<Physics>`: Loads Rapier's wasm on first use (a separate, streamed .wasm file), then builds a world.\n115\t- `world: RAPIER.World`: The Rapier world. A new one per level (`clear()`): read it from here, don't keep it across levels.\n116\t- `alpha: number`: Interpolation factor between the previous and current physics step, 0..1.\n117\t- `steps: number`\n118\t- `readonly fields: ForceFields`: Zones that push, pull or drag (wind, fans, wells, currents): dynamic bodies get them every step, `PlatformerCharacter` reads them (physics/forces.ts).\n119\t- `stepMs: number`: Milliseconds Rapier took for one step (smoothed): what a pile of bodies costs.\n120\t- `update(dt: number, fixedUpdate?: (dt: number) => void): void`: Advance the simulation by real elapsed time. `fixedUpdate` runs before each fixed step (apply forces, move kinematic characters there).\n121\t- `state(): PhysicsState`: The physics as data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): the fixed step's clock and the whole Rapier world, exact. Bodies and colliders keep their handles across `setState`: a game finds its own again with `world.getRigidBody(handle)` / `getCollider(handle)…\n122\t- `setState(s: PhysicsState): void`: Back to `state()`: the world it saved replaces this one, bound meshes re-found by handle.\n123\t- `restoreState(s: PhysicsState): PhysicsRestore`: `setState`, undoable: the saved world is built first (nothing changes if that fails), then replaces this one; `undo()` puts everything back as it was, `commit()` frees the old world. A mesh bound to a body the saved world doesn't have is let go (it stays wher…\n124\t- `get time: number`: Seconds of physics simulated (fixed steps × 1/60): the clock movers run on.\n125\t- `onStep(f: () => void, o: { first?: boolean } = {}): () => void`: Run `f` after every fixed step, before bound meshes (`bind`) read their bodies. Listeners run in the order they were added (instanced bodies keep their last two poses, a ragdoll holds its joint limits); `{ first: true }` runs `f` ahead of all of those, for so…\n126\t- `addMover(body: KinematicBody, options: MoverOptions): Mover`: Drive a kinematic body (`RigidBodyDesc.kinematicPositionBased()`) along a path, spinning or swinging, as a pure function of its own clock (physics/movers.ts), which starts at 0 now: the same however fast frames come. The character rides it. Removing the body…\n127\t- `conveyor(collider: RAPIER.Collider, velocity: readonly [number, number, number] | null): void`: Make a collider a conveyor belt moving at `velocity` (m/s, world axes): dynamic bodies touching it are dragged along, the character standing on it is carried.\n128\t- `beltVelocity(handle: number): readonly [number, number, number] | null`: A conveyor's velocity, by collider handle (null: not a belt).\n129\t- `explode(at: readonly [number, number, number] | Vector3, o: ExplosionOptions = {}): number`: Push every dynamic body near `at` away from it (physics/forces.ts). Returns how many.\n130\t- `bind(body: RAPIER.RigidBody, object: Object3D): void`: Make `object` follow `body` (interpolated).\n131\t- `unbind(object: Object3D): void`\n132\t- `addStaticBox(o: BoxOptions): RAPIER.Collider`\n133\t- `addStaticCylinder(position: [number, number, number], halfHeight: number, radius: number): RAPIER.Collider`\n134\t- `addDynamicBox(o: BoxOptions): RAPIER.RigidBody`\n135\t- `tag(collider: RAPIER.Collider, ...tags: string[]): RAPIER.Collider`: Tag a collider with gameplay semantics the character controller understands: 'climbable' (ladders/vines), 'pushable', 'grabbable', 'slippery', 'noLedge', 'character', 'noCamera' (camera rays pass through). Games may add their own.\n136\t- `hasTag(collider: RAPIER.Collider | null | undefined, tag: string): boolean`\n137\t- `raycast(from: Vector3, dir: Vector3, maxDistance: number, ignoreTags: readonly string[] = NO_TAGS): number | null`: Distance along `dir` (unit) from `from` to the first collider not carrying any of `ignoreTags`.\n138\t- `castRay(from: Vector3, dir: Vector3, maxDistance: number, ignoreTags: readonly string[] = NO_TAGS, exclude?: RAPIER.RigidBody): { distance: number; collider: RAPIER.Collider; normal: Vector3; point: Vector3 } | null`: Ray cast returning distance, hit collider and surface normal.\n139\t- `castUp(x: number, y: number, z: number, maxDistance: number, out: { y: number; nx: number; ny: number; nz: number; id: number }, ignoreTags: readonly string[] = NO_TAGS, exclude?: RAPIER.RigidBody): boolean`: Straight up from (x, y, z), at most `maxDistance` (a ceiling, a lift coming down): like `castDown`, `out.y` is the hit height.\n140\t- `castDown(x: number, y: number, z: number, maxDistance: number, out: { y: number; nx: number; ny: number; nz: number; id: number }, ignoreTags: readonly string[] = NO_TAGS, exclude?: RAPIER.RigidBody): boolean`: Straight down from (x, y, z), at most `maxDistance`, skipping colliders with any of `ignoreTags` (pass a constant array) and `exclude`. Fills `out` (hit height, normal, collider handle) without allocating; \n141\t- `groundBelow(origin: Vector3, maxDistance = 20, exclude?: RAPIER.RigidBody): { y: number; distance: number } | null`: Distance straight down from `origin` to the first collider (excluding `exclude`).\n142\t- `trigger(shape: TriggerShape, at: readonly [number, number, number] | Vector3, options: TriggerOptions = {}): Trigger`: A trigger volume with enter/exit callbacks (pickups, checkpoints, kill zones, doors). Checked after every fixed step against dynamic and kinematic bodies (the character included); set `includeStatic` for level geometry. Never blocks movement or rays.\n143\t- `updateTriggers(): void`: Run every trigger's overlap query now (Physics.update does this after each step).\n144\t- `isAlive(target: { isValid(): boolean }): boolean`: Whether `target` (a body, collider or joint) still exists in the current world: false once removed, and for anything from a level before (its world is gone; `isValid()` would throw).\n145\t- `remove(target: RAPIER.RigidBody | RAPIER.Collider): void`: Remove a rigid body (with its colliders and any bound Object3D binding) or a single collider. Removing a collider whose fixed body has no other colliders removes that body too (what `addStaticBox` & co. create). Tags and trigger overlaps are cleaned up.\n146\t- `clear(): void`: Empty the world: every body, collider, joint, character and vehicle controller, trigger, tag, binding, mover, field and belt goes; gravity and the solver's iterations go back to their defaults, the step accumulator and the step count reset. `world` is a new R…\n147\t- `fingerprint(): string`: A hash of the physics state: every body (position, rotation, velocities, asleep, enabled) and collider (pose, friction, restitution, sensor, enabled), exact, plus the step count and the time not yet stepped. Two runs that should be the same compare equal only…\n148\t- `counts(): { bodies: number; colliders: number; joints: number; tags: number; bindings: number; triggers: number; controllers: number; vehicles: number; movers: number; fields: number; belts: number; }`: Sizes of everything Physics tracks (leak checks, debug UI).\n149\t- `dispose(): void`\n150\t\n151\t### interface LightRequestOptions\n152\t\n153\t- `position?: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]`: World position (copied). Ignored while `follow` is set (then it is `follow` + `offset`).\n154\t- `follow?: Object3D | null`: Follow an object's world position every frame (a projectile, a monster, the hero).\n155\t- `offset?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Offset from `follow` (world axes).\n156\t- `color?: ColorRepresentation`\n157\t- `intensity?: number`: Point light intensity at full strength (before flicker and fades). Default 4.\n158\t- `radius?: number`: Reach in metres (PointLight.distance: light is 0 beyond it). Default 6.\n159\t- `decay?: number`: Distance falloff exponent (PointLight.decay; a uniform, free to change). Default 1.2.\n160\t- `flicker?: FlickerPreset`\n161\t- `priority?: number`: Importance multiplier: the hero's light 4, a loot beam 2, a torch 1 (default).\n162\t- `fadeIn?: number`: Fade times in seconds (defaults 0.25 in, 0.2 out).\n163\t- `fadeOut?: number`\n164\t- `lifetime?: number`: Release automatically after this many seconds (spell flashes, explosions).\n165\t- `name?: string`: Optional label for debugging (`pool.describe()`).\n166\t\n167\t### interface LightHandle\n168\t\n169\tA light request. Change it freely; the pool decides whether it currently owns a real light.\n170\t\n171\t- `readonly id: number`\n172\t- `readonly position: Vector3`: World position (mutable; ignored while following).\n173\t- `readonly color: Color`: Colour (mutable).\n174\t- `intensity: number`\n175\t- `radius: number`\n176\t- `decay: number`\n177\t- `flicker: FlickerPreset`\n178\t- `priority: number`\n179\t- `follow: Object3D | null`\n180\t- `readonly offset: Vector3`\n181\t- `name: string`\n182\t- `lifetime: number`: Seconds left before auto-release (Infinity = until `release()`).\n183\t- `update(options: LightRequestOptions): this`: Set several fields at once.\n184\t- `release(): void`: Fade out and give the light back. Safe to call twice.\n185\t- `readonly alive: boolean`\n186\t- `readonly lit: boolean`: Whether it holds one of the pool's real lights right now.\n187\t- `readonly level: number`: 0..1: how much of it is shown (fades).\n188\t\n189\t### class Particles\n190\t\n191\tPixel-art particles: CPU-simulated pools, drawn as camera-facing square sprites in flat\n192\tpalette colors. One emitter (one instanced draw call, one pooled instance buffer) per\n193\tpreset; sizes are whole art pixels, so in Pixel mode a particle is a crisp N×N block.\n194\tAvailable as `ctx.particles`; emitters are created on first use and freed by `clear()`\n195\t(the engine calls it when a level unloads).\n196\t\n197\t  ctx.particles.burst('dust', hero.feet);\n198\t  ctx.particles.burst('skid', feet, { direction: [-vx, 0.3, -vz], count: 2 });\n199\t\n200\t- `new Particles(view: () => ParticleView)`\n201\t- `readonly group: Group<Object3DEventMap>`: Engine-owned group holding every emitter.\n202\t- `readonly presets: Map<string, ParticlePreset>`: Preset registry: the built-ins plus anything registered.\n203\t- `readonly pixelWorld: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`: World units per art pixel at the camera focus (updated every frame).\n204\t- `register(name: string, preset: ParticlePreset): void`: Add or replace a named preset. Replacing frees the old preset's emitter.\n205\t- `burst(preset: string | ParticlePreset, at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number], options: BurstOptions = {}): number`: Spawn a burst of `preset` (a name or a ParticlePreset) at `at`. Returns the number of particles spawned (fewer when the pool is full, 0 for an unknown preset). Inline preset objects share one emitter per distinct content, so `burst({ ...literal }, at)` every…\n206\t- `get alive: number`: Particles alive right now, over all emitters.\n207\t- `get emitterCount: number`: Emitters (= draw calls at most) currently allocated.\n208\t- `update(dt: number): void`: Simulate and upload. Called by the engine every frame (frozen while paused).\n209\t- `reseed(rng: Rng): void`: Take bursts' randomness from `rng` (the engine passes each level's `ctx.random` fork).\n210\t- `clear(): void`: Kill every particle and free the emitters (GPU buffers, materials).\n211\t- `dispose(): void`\n212\t\n213\t=== interface ParticleDef\\|interface ParticleConfig\\|interface EmitterConfig NOT FOUND\n214\t### interface ClipDef\n215\t\n216\t- `name: string`\n217\t- `frames: number`: Length in frames at 30 fps.\n218\t- `loop?: boolean`\n219\t- `keys: readonly Key[]`\n220\t- `layers?: readonly Layer[]`\n221\t- `feet?: readonly FeetKey[]`: Feet track: foot goals interpolated between its own keys and solved with IK every frame, overriding the keyed leg rotations. Use it whenever feet touch the floor through a transition (kneel → stand, sit → lie) so they roll instead of sinking.\n222\t- `speed?: number`: Ground speed (m/s) the clip is authored for at playback rate 1. Locomotion clips set this; metrics then measure foot sliding against it and the runtime can scale playback.\n223\t- `stance?: number`: Gait cycles (gaitClip): the share of the cycle each foot is planted. The right heel strikes at phase 0, the left at 0.5, so the right foot is mid-stance at `stance / 2`.\n224\t- `reach?: number`: Gaits: how far ahead of the hips (m) the middle of the stance is (feet touch down `reach` + half a stance's ground ahead).\n225\t- `grounded?: boolean`: Grounded clip: soles should rest on y = 0 (metrics flag floating / penetration).\n226\t- `fast?: boolean`: Intentionally snappy (flips, punches, launches): skip the angular-speed warning.\n227\t- `notes?: string`\n228\t\n229\t### compileClips()\n230\t\n231\t```ts\n232\tfunction compileClips(defs: readonly ClipDef[], rig: RigSpec, model: Object3D): AnimationClip[]\n233\t```\n234\t\n235\t### HERO_RIG\n236\t\n237\t```ts\n238\tconst HERO_RIG: RigSpec\n239\t```\n240\t\n241\tThe hero rig (public/assets/hero.glb, built by scripts/assets/hero.mjs).\n242\t\n243\tThe character faces +Z. L/R are the character's own sides (its right = -X).\n244\tRotations are Euler XYZ degrees relative to rest; Z is applied first, then Y, then X,\n245\tall about the parent's axes. Verified against contact sheets (`npm run anim -- sheet`).\n246\t\n247\t  Pelvis   +X tip forward (90 = face down, -90 = on its back, 360 = front flip)\n248\t           +Y turn to its left, +Z roll to its RIGHT. p = [x, y, z] offset (m), s = squash\n249\t  Torso    +X bend forward, +Y twist left, +Z lean right\n250\t  Head     +X look down, +Y look left, +Z tilt right\n251\t  Arm*     X swings: -90 = straight ahead, -180 = overhead, +40 = back\n252\t           Z spreads (…\n253\t\n254\t## 14. three.js and Rapier\n255\t\n256\t### HERO_RAGDOLL\n257\t\n258\t```ts\n259\tconst HERO_RAGDOLL: readonly RagdollPart[]\n260\t```\n261\t\n262\tThe hero as a ragdoll (the joints of `hero.glb`, see rig.ts): pelvis, torso and head, upper\n263\tarms and legs on ball joints held in cones, elbows and knees as hinges bending the way the\n264\tclips bend them (Forearm −X, Shin +X). Hands and feet ride on their forearm and shin.\n265\t\n266\t### skyAt()\n267\t\n268\t```ts\n269\tfunction skyAt(hour: number): SkyState\n270\t```\n271\t\n272\tThe sky at `hour` (wraps around 24).\n273\t\n274\t### applySky()\n275\t\n276\t```ts\n277\tfunction applySky(engine: Pick<Engine, 'sun' | 'ambient' | 'scene' | 'setSunDirection'>, s: SkyState): void\n278\t```\n279\t\n280\tPut a sky on the engine: sun direction, colour and strength, ambient light, background.\n281\t\n282\t### groundFog()\n283\t\n284\t```ts\n285\tfunction groundFog(o: { color?: number; top?: number; amount?: number; near?: number; far?: number } = {}): { node: Node; color: UniformNode<\"color\", Color>; amount: UniformNode<\"float\", number>; top: UniformNode<\"float\", number>; }\n286\t```\n287\t\n288\tFog for `scene.fogNode` with live settings (uniforms: changing them never recompiles):\n289\tground mist that thickens below `top` (m) and a haze that grows with distance from the\n290\tcamera, both up to `amount` (0..1), in `color` (match the sky).\n291\t\n292\t  const mist = groundFog({ color: s.sky, top: 1.5 });\n293\t  (scene as { fogNode: unknown }).fogNode = mist.node;\n294\t  mist.amount.value = 0.8; mist.color.value.setHex(s.sky);\n295\t\n296\t### gaitClip()\n297\t\n298\t```ts\n299\tfunction gaitClip(rig: RigSpec, g: GaitSpec): ClipDef\n300\t```\n301\t\n302\t### interface RigSpec\n303\t\n304\tEverything the tools need to know about a rig beyond its hierarchy.\n305\t\n306\t- `joints: readonly string[]`: Joint names in hierarchy order (root first).\n307\t- `root: string`\n308\t- `mirror: Readonly<Record<string, string>>`: Left/right counterparts for mirroring.\n309\t- `soles: readonly string[]`: Mesh names that touch the ground (soles).\n310\t- `trace: readonly string[]`: Points to trace in contact sheets (joint or mesh names).\n311\t- `limits: Readonly<Record<string, JointLimit>>`: Human-plausible rotation ranges, degrees.\n312\t- `fps: number`: Frame rate of the authoring format.\n313\t- `legs?: LegRig`: Leg geometry, enabling foot IK and the gait generator.\n314\t- `spine?: { torso?: string; head?: string; cap?: string }`: Joints the runtime procedural layers turn (PoseLayers): the torso and head look where the character is going; `cap` is an optional springy joint (not animated by clips).\n315\t\n316\t### renderSound()\n317\t\n318\t```ts\n319\tfunction renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate = 44100, seed = 1): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>\n320\t```\n321\t\n322\tRender a sound to mono samples in -1..1.\n323\t\n324\t### spatialize()\n325\t\n326\t```ts\n327\tfunction spatialize(listener: Listener, at: V3Like, o: SpatialOptions = {}): Spatial\n328\t```\n329\t\n330\tGain and pan of a sound at `at` for `listener`.\n331\t\n332\t### interface Voice\n333\t\n334\tA looping sound steered while it plays (`AudioManager.loop`): a hum, a fire, a waterfall.\n335\t\n336\t- `readonly name: string`\n337\t- `readonly settings: Readonly<Required<VoiceSettings>>`: The values last set (kept headless too, so games and tests can read them).\n338\t- `readonly sounding: boolean`: Really sounding: Web Audio running and the loop started.\n339\t- `readonly stopped: boolean`\n340\t- `set(s: VoiceSettings): void`: Change any of its settings; they glide over a few hundredths of a second (no clicks).\n341\t- `stop(): void`\n342\t\n343\t### class FirstPersonRig\n344\t\n345\tEyes of the character: mouse look (click to lock the pointer) or Q/E to turn. No zoom.\n346\t\n347\t- `new FirstPersonRig(config: CameraConfig = {})`\n348\t- `readonly preset: \"first\"`\n349\t- `readonly camera: PerspectiveCamera`\n350\t- `readonly zoomable: false`: Whether zoom applies (all presets except first person).\n351\t- `readonly hidesTarget: true`: First person hides the player model.\n352\t- `yaw: number`\n353\t- `pitch: number`\n354\t- `groundBasis(): { right: Vector3; forward: Vector3; }`: Screen-relative movement basis on the ground plane.\n355\t- `describe(): { yaw: number; pitch: number; }`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n356\t\n357\t### class CameraRig\n358\t\n359\tBase rig: owns the three.js camera, zoom and the ground-plane movement basis.\n360\t\n361\t- `new CameraRig(config: CameraConfig)`\n362\t- `readonly preset: CameraPreset`\n363\t- `readonly camera: OrthographicCamera | PerspectiveCamera`\n364\t- `readonly focus: Vector3`: World point the rig is centred on (used for shadow framing).\n365\t- `zoom: number`\n366\t- `readonly minZoom: number`\n367\t- `readonly maxZoom: number`\n368\t- `readonly zoomable: boolean`: Whether zoom applies (all presets except first person).\n369\t- `readonly hidesTarget: boolean`: First person hides the player model.\n370\t- `readonly lockDepth: boolean`: Side-scroller: characters should stay on their depth lane.\n371\t- `controlsCharacter: boolean`: False while the free camera is flying (input drives the camera, not the player).\n372\t- `snap: boolean`: Pixel-snap the camera (ortho only).\n373\t- `aspect: number`: Viewport aspect (width / height), following the art resolution (see `setAspect`).\n374\t- `rayAt(x: number, y: number, origin: Vector3, dir: Vector3): void`: The ray through screen point (x, y) in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up, as `input.pointer` gives them): where it starts (on the near plane) and its unit direction. For picking things with the mouse, or aiming at it. An orthographic rig stands far b…\n375\t- `setZoom(zoom: number): void`\n376\t- `setAspect(aspect: number): void`: Match the art resolution's aspect (adaptive framing changes it with the screen). `update()` calls this from `CameraUpdate.resolution`; the engine also calls it on swap.\n377\t- `teleport(target: Vector3): void`: Place the camera without smoothing.\n378\t- `groundBasis(): { right: Vector3; forward: Vector3 }`: Screen-relative movement basis on the ground plane.\n379\t- `update(u: CameraUpdate): void`\n380\t- `describe(): Record<string, unknown>`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n381\t- `state(): CameraRigState`: The rig as data, exact (`engine.snapshot()`).\n382\t- `setState(s: CameraRigState): void`: Put the rig back where `state()` found it (the same preset).\n383\t\n384\t### withUrlOptions()\n385\t\n386\t```ts\n387\tfunction withUrlOptions(options: EngineOptions, search = location.search): EngineOptions\n388\t```\n389\t\n390\tA game's own options with the review flags from the page URL (optionsFromUrl) on top:\n391\t`Engine.start(game, withUrlOptions({ container, camera: { preset: 'side', zoom: 1.2 } }))`.\n392\tThe camera is merged key by key (`?zoom=1.5` keeps the game's `side` preset), and\n393\t`?filters=` replaces the game's look (a look would win over filters otherwise).\n394\t\n395\t## 2. Camera\n396\t\n397\t### class Rng\n398\t\n399\tSeeded randomness. Everything random in a game comes from an `Rng`, so the same seed always\n400\tgives the same result (docs/DOCTRINE.md, principle 1). Each level gets one, `ctx.random`,\n401\tseeded from the engine's seed and the game's name; fork it by purpose so streams stay\n402\tindependent of how many numbers each one drew:\n403\t\n404\t  const level = ctx.random.fork(`level:${n}`);\n405\t  const room = level.fork(`room:${i}`);   // independent of how many numbers `level` drew\n406\t\n407\tNever use Math.random in game or engine code (the lint refuses it).\n408\t\n409\t- `new Rng(seed: number | string)`\n410\t- `get state: number`: The generator's whole state: save it, and set it back to draw the same numbers again.\n411\t- `next(): number`: Uniform in [0, 1). mulberry32.\n412\t- `fork(purpose: string | number): Rng`: A child generator for one purpose; does not advance this one.\n413\t- `range(min: number, max: number): number`: Float in [min, max).\n414\t- `int(min: number, max: number): number`: Integer in [min, max] (inclusive).\n415\t- `chance(p: number): boolean`\n416\t- `pick(items: readonly T[]): T`\n417\t- `weighted(items: readonly T[], weight: (item: T) => number): T`: Pick by weight; entries with weight <= 0 never come up.\n418\t- `shuffle(items: T[]): T[]`\n419\t- `gaussian(mean = 0, sd = 1): number`: Normal distribution (Box–Muller).\n420\t\n421\t### swayObject()\n422\t\n423\t```ts\n424\tfunction swayObject(root: Object3D, wind: WindUniforms, o: SwayOptions): void\n425\t```\n426\t\n427\tMake every mesh under `root` sway (its materials replaced by swaying copies).\n428\t\n429\t### class WindUniforms\n430\t\n431\t- `readonly time: any`\n432\t- `readonly wind: any`\n433\t- `update(time: number, wind: Vector2 | readonly [number, number]): void`: Per frame (game time); `wind` along x and z (its length is the strength).\n434\t\n435\t### clothGrid()\n436\t\n437\t```ts\n438\tfunction clothGrid(o: ClothOptions): VerletBody & { cols: number; rows: number; uv: Float32Array }\n439\t```\n440\t\n441\tA sheet of cloth: a grid of particles with structural, shear and bend edges, two triangles per cell.\n442\t\n443\t### class SoftMesh\n444\t\n445\t- `new SoftMesh(body: VerletBody, material: Material, o: { uv?: Float32Array; doubleSided?: boolean } = {})`\n446\t- `readonly body: VerletBody`\n447\t- `sync(): void`: Copy the particles into the mesh (call once per frame after stepping the body).\n448\t\n449\t### interface WaterOptions\n450\t\n451\t- `size: readonly [number, number]`: Width (x) and depth (z) of the surface, m.\n452\t- `at: readonly [number, number, number]`: Centre of the surface at rest.\n453\t- `segments?: readonly [number, number]`: Grid quads along x and z (default: one per ripple cell, or 4 per metre without ripples).\n454\t- `waves?: readonly Wave[]`\n455\t- `ripples?: RippleField | null`: A ripple field covering the same rectangle (its size and centre must match).\n456\t- `deep?: number`: Colours (hex): troughs, crests, foam.\n457\t- `shallow?: number`\n458\t- `foam?: number`\n459\t- `foamAt?: number`: Height above rest (m) where foam starts. Default: 75% of the waves' combined height (the tallest crests).\n460\t- `opacity?: number`: 1: opaque; less: see-through by a 4×4 ordered dither per art pixel.\n461\t\n462\t### interface SpriteOptions\n463\t\n464\t- `size?: number`: Height in metres (the width follows the frame's aspect). Default 1.\n465\t- `frame?: number`\n466\t- `flip?: boolean`: Mirror left-right (a sprite facing the other way).\n467\t\n468\t### spriteSheet()\n469\t\n470\t```ts\n471\tfunction spriteSheet(idOrDef: string | TextureDef, opts: TextureOptions = {}): SpriteSheet\n472\t```\n473\t\n474\tA sprite sheet for `SpriteBatch`: the def's frames left to right in one row, rows stored top\n475\tfirst (`flipY` false, which SpriteBatch reads). Not cached: a SpriteBatch disposes its sheet.\n476\t\n477\t### textureDef()\n478\t\n479\t```ts\n480\tfunction textureDef(def: T): T\n481\t```\n482\t\n483\tReturns the def as written, typed, so tuples and literals don't widen (as `model` does). Not\n484\t`texture`: TSL's `texture()` samples a map, and games import both.\n485\t\n486\t### class Trail\n487\t\n488\t- `new Trail(o: TrailOptions = {})`\n489\t- `readonly capacity: number`\n490\t- `width: number`\n491\t- `life: number`\n492\t- `readonly head: Color`: Head and tail colours (live: `trail.head.setHex(...)`).\n493\t- `readonly tail: Color`\n494\t- `push(p: Vector3, time: number): void`: The trail's newest point (call every frame while it moves).\n495\t- `reset(): void`: Drop every point.\n496\t- `get length: number`: Points alive now.\n497\t- `update(time: number, camera: Camera): void`: Per frame: forget old points, rebuild the ribbon facing `camera`.\n498\t- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n499\t\n500\t### class Decals\n501\t\n502\t- `new Decals(scene: Scene, o: { capacity?: number } = {})`\n503\t- `readonly meshes: Map<\"ring\" | \"crack\" | \"footprint\" | \"scorch\" | \"splat\", InstancedMesh<BufferGeometry<NormalBufferAttributes, BufferGeometryEventMap>, Material<...> | Material…`\n504\t- `readonly o: { capacity?: number }`\n505\t- `add(shape: DecalShape, at: Vector3Like, normal: Vector3Like, o: DecalOptions = {}): number`: Lay a mark at `at` on a surface facing `normal`. Returns its index in that shape's pool.\n506\t- `count(shape?: DecalShape): number`: Marks alive (per shape or all).\n507\t- `update(dt: number): void`: Per frame: age the marks; old ones dither away over a second.\n508\t- `clear(): void`: Remove every mark (the meshes stay for reuse).\n509\t- `dispose(): void`\n510\t\n511\t### class Boids\n512\t\n513\t- `new Boids(count: number, o: BoidOptions = {})`\n514\t- `readonly count: number`\n515\t- `readonly pos: Float32Array`\n516\t- `readonly vel: Float32Array`\n517\t- `o: Required<Omit<BoidOptions, 'bounds' | 'seed'>> & { bounds: BoidOptions['bounds'] }`\n518\t- `seek: Vec3 | null`: A point everyone is drawn toward (null: none).\n519\t- `seekWeight: number`\n520\t- `flee: { at: Vec3; radius: number } | null`: A threat to scatter from.\n521\t- `fleeWeight: number`\n522\t- `obstacles: { x: number; y: number; z: number; r: number }[]`: Spheres to steer around (x, y, z, r).\n523\t- `spacing(): number`: Average distance to the nearest neighbour (tests, tools).\n524\t- `order(): { speed: number; alignment: number }`: Average speed and how lined up the headings are (0 random, 1 all the same way).\n525\t- `step(dt: number): void`\n526\t\n527\t### class BehaviorTree\n528\t\n529\t- `new BehaviorTree(root: BtNode<B>, blackboard: B)`\n530\t- `ticks: number`: Ticks so far.\n531\t- `time: number`: Seconds ticked so far (the sum of every dt).\n532\t- `status: Status | null`\n533\t- `readonly root: BtNode<B>`\n534\t- `readonly blackboard: B`\n535\t- `tick(dt: number): Status`\n536\t- `halt(): void`: Stop everything (a cutscene, a stun): running actions are told to halt.\n537\t- `trace(): TraceRow[]`: Every node depth-first with this tick's answer: for drawing the tree.\n538\t- `activePath(): string[]`: The names down the branch that decided this tick, root first (the last child each node ran that didn't fail).\n539\t\n540\t### bt\n541\t\n542\t```ts\n543\tconst bt: { readonly selector: <B>(name: string, ...children: BtNode<B>[]) => BtNode<B>; readonly sequence: <B>(name: string, ...children: BtNode<B>[]) => BtNode<B>; ... 8 more ...; readonly cooldown: <B>(child: BtNode<...>, seconds: number, name?: string) => BtNode<...>; }\n544\t```\n545\t\n546\tThe builders in one object, for `bt.selector(...)` without importing each.\n547\t\n548\t### interface TimelineOptions\n549\t\n550\t- `shots?: readonly CameraKey[]`\n551\t- `cues?: readonly Cue[]`\n552\t- `lines?: readonly Line[]`\n553\t- `duration?: number`: Length (s); default the last key, cue or line.\n554\t- `bars?: number`: Seconds the letterbox bars take to slide in and out (default 0.6; 0: no bars).\n555\t- `onEnd?: () => void`: Called once when the playhead reaches the end (or the scene is skipped).\n556\t\n557\t### buildModel()\n558\t\n559\t```ts\n560\tfunction buildModel(idOrDef: string | ModelDef, opts: ModelOptions = {}): ModelBuild\n561\t```\n562\t\n563\t### addModelColliders()\n564\t\n565\t```ts\n566\tfunction addModelColliders(physics: Physics, build: Pick<ModelBuild, 'colliders'>, at: Vec3 = [0, 0, 0], yaw = 0): RAPIER.Collider[]\n567\t```\n568\t\n569\tStatic Rapier colliders for a placed model (its `colliders` data), at `at` turned `yaw`\n570\tradians about Y: a box, an upright cylinder or a convex hull, with `addStaticBox`'s friction.\n571\t\n572\t### class ModelCatalog\n573\t\n574\tModels by id: the engine's built-in library (`MODEL_LIBRARY`) or a game's own. `use` parts\n575\tand `buildModel('id')` resolve ids through one (the library unless a build passes another);\n576\ta game adds its models to the library, or builds a catalog of its own on top of it.\n577\t\n578\t- `new ModelCatalog(defs: readonly ModelDef[] = [])`\n579\t\n580\t=== interface BuildOptions\\|interface BuildModelOptions NOT FOUND\n581\t### glowMaterial()\n582\t\n583\t```ts\n584\tfunction glowMaterial(color: ColorRepresentation): MeshBasicNodeMaterial\n585\t```\n586\t\n587\tUnlit, full-bright material for a colour (flames, eyes, screens, crystal cores), shared and\n588\tcached per colour like `toonMaterial` (`userData.shared`; disposing it drops it from the\n589\tcache). Its vertices snap with the toon materials' under the `ps1` filter, so a glowing part\n590\tstays on its model.\n591\t\n592\t### interface ToonMaterialOptions\n593\t\n594\t- `map?: Texture | null`: Base color texture. Switched to nearest filtering (crisp texels, no mip blur).\n595\t- `vertexColors?: boolean`: Multiply by the geometry's `color` attribute (vertex colors).\n596\t- `rim?: number`: A hard-banded rim light in this colour (hex) on grazing, upward-facing surfaces: a silhouette that reads against dark floors (monsters in dark levels). Added to the emissive, so `material.emissive` (hit flashes) still works on top. Same shader for every rim c…\n597\t- `dissolve?: boolean`: Pixel dissolve: the material reads its own `dissolve` property (0 = solid, 1 = gone) and discards a per-art-pixel noise pattern below it, with a 1-pixel glowing front in the rim colour (or white). Set it on per-body clones (`material.dissolve = 0.4`), not on…\n598\t\n599\t## 5. Physics\n600\t\n601\t### checkModel()\n602\t\n603\t```ts\n604\tfunction checkModel(idOrDef: string | ModelDef, opts: CheckOptions = {}): ModelCheck\n605\t```\n606\t\n607\tEvery check over the model's range: the defaults, then each param at its min and at its max\n608\t(one at a time), each at seeds 1, 2 and 3 when the parts read the rng. Messages say which\n609\tbuild they came from unless every build agrees.\n610\t\n611\t### validateTexture()\n612\t\n613\t```ts\n614\tfunction validateTexture(def: TextureDef): TextureCheck\n615\t```\n616\t\n617\t### mergeStaticMeshes()\n618\t\n619\t```ts\n620\tfunction mergeStaticMeshes(sources: readonly Object3D[], options: MergeOptions = {}): Mesh[]\n621\t```\n622\t\n623\tMerge static meshes into one mesh per (material, shadow flags): N blocks with M\n624\tmaterials become ≤ M draw calls instead of N × groups (a BoxGeometry with 6 material\n625\tgroups is 6 draws, plus 6 more in the shadow pass).\n626\t\n627\tWorld transforms are baked into the vertices, multi-material geometry is split by its\n628\tgroups, and `position` + `normal` are kept, plus `uv` under a material with a `map` and\n629\t`color` under one with `vertexColors` (what toon materials read). The\n630\tsources are left untouched (don't add them to the scene). Physics is separate: keep\n631\tone collider per block. Merged meshes are culled as a whole, so merge things that are\n632\ton screen together (a level, a room), and keep anything that moves…\n633\t\n634\t### encodeState()\n635\t\n636\t```ts\n637\tfunction encodeState(v: unknown): unknown\n638\t```\n639\t\n640\tA deep copy of `v` as plain JSON that `JSON.stringify` keeps exactly: `-0`, `Infinity`,\n641\t`-Infinity` and `NaN` become the strings `'@-0'`, `'@Infinity'`... (JSON would write 0 and null,\n642\tand `atan2(-0, x)` is not `atan2(0, x)`), and a string of the game's own that starts with `@`\n643\tgets one more (`'@x'` → `'@@x'`). `decodeState` turns them back. `engine.snapshot()` and\n644\t`restore()` do both; a game's `snapshot()` returns its values as they are (never through JSON).\n645\t\n646\t### interface PrecipitationOptions\n647\t\n648\t- `kind: PrecipitationKind`\n649\t- `count: number`: Most drops at once (the instance count).\n650\t- `area?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Width, height and depth of the air the drops fall through, around the centre.\n651\t- `color?: number`: Drop colour (hex).\n652\t- `speed?: number`: Fall speed (m/s). Rain 16, snow 1.4.\n653\t\n654\t### class ScreenFx\n655\t\n656\t- `cover(kind: TransitionKind = 'fade', o: TransitionOptions = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Cover the picture (progress → 1). Resolves when it stops: `true` when it is fully covered, `false` when another transition (or `set`, `clear`) took over first.\n657\t- `reveal(kind?: TransitionKind, o: TransitionOptions = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Uncover it (progress → 0) with `kind` (default: the one that covered it). Resolves like `cover`.\n658\t- `transition(kind: TransitionKind, between: () => unknown, o: TransitionOptions & { revealCenter?: TransitionOptions['center'] } = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Cover, run `between` (load a level, teleport), then reveal, also when `between` throws (the error is passed on). Resolves to whether the cover completed.\n659\t- `set(kind: TransitionKind | null, progress: number): void`: Jump straight to a progress (0 clear, 1 covered), no animation.\n660\t- `flash(color: ScreenColor = 'white', o: FlashOptions = {}): void`: A full-screen flash that fades out.\n661\t- `shockwave(at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number], o: ShockwaveOptions = {}): void`: A ring that pushes the picture outwards from `at` (oldest ring is replaced when 4 run).\n662\t- `clear(): void`: Stop everything (a clear picture).\n663\t- `state(): ScreenFxState`\n664\t- `update(dt: number, camera: Camera | null): void`: Engine, every rendered frame (real seconds; `camera` projects world centres).\n665\t- `sampleNode(tex: any, scale: any): any`: Sample an art-resolution picture for the device pass, pushed by the shockwaves and blocked up by a mosaic transition, with the rings' bright edge. `tex` is the art target (a texture node); `scale` is device pixels per art pixel.\n666\t- `colorNode(color: any, scale: any): any`: The transition and the flash over a finished colour (device pass).\n667\t\n668\t### class CameraShake\n669\t\n670\t- `trauma: number`\n671\t- `maxOffset: number`: World units at full trauma.\n672\t- `decay: number`: Trauma lost per second.\n673\t- `strength: number`: Global multiplier (settings: 0 turns shake off).\n674\t- `readonly offset: Vector3`\n675\t- `state(): ShakeState`: Trauma and the noise clock, as data (`engine.snapshot()`): what the next frames shake by follows from them.\n676\t- `setState(s: ShakeState): void`\n677\t- `add(amount: number): void`\n678\t- `update(dt: number): Vector3`: Advance and compute `offset`.\n679\t- `apply(rig: { readonly focus: Vector3 }, dt: number): void`: Update and move a camera rig's focus by the change in offset since the last call.\n680\t- `reset(): void`\n681\t\n682\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"71ec49de-27ff-45fb-a784-9f3cf302f915","uuid":"fcfea9d7-fd4c-4cf5-b3d3-97d0005bcd0f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:38.051Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-build-v2--20261105-162027--x07393c-work/71ec49de-27ff-45fb-a784-9f3cf302f915/tool-results/bm7q00qmy.txt","content":"### interface TerrainOptions\n\n- `size?: readonly [number, number]`: Size in metres along x and z (default [32, 32]).\n- `cells?: readonly [number, number]`: Cells along x and z (default one per metre).\n- `at?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Where the middle of the grid's height 0 is (default the origin).\n- `bands?: readonly TerrainBand[]`: Colour by height, lowest first; the last band takes everything above.\n- `steep?: number`: Faces steeper than this (their normal's y below it) are rock. Default 0.72 (about 44°).\n- `rock?: number`\n\n### interface GrassOptions\n\n- `area: readonly [number, number]`: Width (x) and depth (z) of the patch, m.\n- `at: readonly [number, number, number]`: Centre of the patch on the ground.\n- `count: number`\n- `height?: readonly [number, number]`: Blade heights (m): shortest and tallest. Default [0.25, 0.55].\n- `width?: number`: Blade width at the root (m). Default 0.07.\n- `base?: number`: Colours (hex): roots and tips.\n- `tip?: number`\n- `mask?: (x: number, z: number) => boolean`: Where blades grow (world x, z); default everywhere in the patch.\n- `ground?: (x: number, z: number) => number`: Ground height under a blade (default: the patch's y).\n- `seed?: number`\n\n### interface CharacterOptions\n\n- `position: [number, number, number]`\n- `radius?: number`\n- `halfHeight?: number`: Height of the cylindrical part of the capsule.\n- `speed?: number`\n- `jumpSpeed?: number`\n- `gravity?: number`\n\n### interface VehicleOptions\n\n- `at: readonly [number, number, number]`\n- `yaw?: number`: Heading (radians about Y; 0 drives toward +Z).\n- `size?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Chassis half extents (m). Default [0.9, 0.35, 1.8].\n- `mass?: number`: Chassis mass in world units. Default 8.\n- `wheelRadius?: number`\n- `suspension?: number`: Suspension rest length and stiffness.\n- `stiffness?: number`\n- `power?: number`: Forward drive force per driven wheel at full throttle. Default 30.\n- `topSpeed?: number`: The engine gives out toward this speed (m/s). Default 16.\n- `steer?: number`: Most steering angle (radians). Default 0.55.\n- `grip?: number`: Grip (friction slip): how much sideways force the tyres hold before sliding. Default 2.2.\n- `driftYaw?: number`: Most spin (rad/s) the handbrake's drift push builds up. Default 2.4; 0 turns the assist off.\n\n### interface DriveInput\n\n- `throttle: number`: -1 (reverse) … 1\n- `steer: number`: -1 (left) … 1 (right)\n- `brake?: boolean`\n- `handbrake?: boolean`\n\n### interface SoundDef\n\n- `wave: Wave`\n- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n- `sustain?: number`\n- `decay?: number`\n- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n- `arpRate?: number`\n- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n\n### interface Song\n\nA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\nwhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\nEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\nnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n\n- `bpm: number`\n- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n\n### interface VoiceSettings\n\n- `volume?: number`: 0..1 (default 1).\n- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n- `muffle?: number`: 0 (clear) .. 1 (behind a thick wall, under water): a low-pass filter closing down.\n- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones.\n\n### interface PlayOptions\n\n- `volume?: number`: 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume.\n- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones (playback rate), e.g. a rising combo.\n- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n\n### interface HudOptions\n\n- `color?: HudColor`: Default 'white'.\n- `shadow?: HudColor | false`: 1-pixel drop shadow color, or false. Default 'ink'.\n- `scale?: number`: Integer art-pixel scale (2 = double-size glyphs). Default 1.\n- `anchor?: HudAnchor`: Default 'top-left': x/y are offsets from that screen corner/edge, in art pixels.\n\n### class Physics\n\nRapier world with a fixed 60 Hz step and render interpolation.\n\nPhysics owns the truth; visuals follow. Bound Object3Ds are *interpolated* between\nthe last two physics states for smooth motion. Pixel alignment is purely a camera /\npresentation concern and never touches bodies here.\n\n- `static create(gravity = -24): Promise<Physics>`: Loads Rapier's wasm on first use (a separate, streamed .wasm file), then builds a world.\n- `world: RAPIER.World`: The Rapier world. A new one per level (`clear()`): read it from here, don't keep it across levels.\n- `alpha: number`: Interpolation factor between the previous and current physics step, 0..1.\n- `steps: number`\n- `readonly fields: ForceFields`: Zones that push, pull or drag (wind, fans, wells, currents): dynamic bodies get them every step, `PlatformerCharacter` reads them (physics/forces.ts).\n- `stepMs: number`: Milliseconds Rapier took for one step (smoothed): what a pile of bodies costs.\n- `update(dt: number, fixedUpdate?: (dt: number) => void): void`: Advance the simulation by real elapsed time. `fixedUpdate` runs before each fixed step (apply forces, move kinematic characters there).\n- `state(): PhysicsState`: The physics as data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): the fixed step's clock and the whole Rapier world, exact. Bodies and colliders keep their handles across `setState`: a game finds its own again with `world.getRigidBody(handle)` / `getCollider(handle)…\n- `setState(s: PhysicsState): void`: Back to `state()`: the world it saved replaces this one, bound meshes re-found by handle.\n- `restoreState(s: PhysicsState): PhysicsRestore`: `setState`, undoable: the saved world is built first (nothing changes if that fails), then replaces this one; `undo()` puts everything back as it was, `commit()` frees the old world. A mesh bound to a body the saved world doesn't have is let go (it stays wher…\n- `get time: number`: Seconds of physics simulated (fixed steps × 1/60): the clock movers run on.\n- `onStep(f: () => void, o: { first?: boolean } = {}): () => void`: Run `f` after every fixed step, before bound meshes (`bind`) read their bodies. Listeners run in the order they were added (instanced bodies keep their last two poses, a ragdoll holds its joint limits); `{ first: true }` runs `f` ahead of all of those, for so…\n- `addMover(body: KinematicBody, options: MoverOptions): Mover`: Drive a kinematic body (`RigidBodyDesc.kinematicPositionBased()`) along a path, spinning or swinging, as a pure function of its own clock (physics/movers.ts), which starts at 0 now: the same however fast frames come. The character rides it. Removing the body…\n- `conveyor(collider: RAPIER.Collider, velocity: readonly [number, number, number] | null): void`: Make a collider a conveyor belt moving at `velocity` (m/s, world axes): dynamic bodies touching it are dragged along, the character standing on it is carried.\n- `beltVelocity(handle: number): readonly [number, number, number] | null`: A conveyor's velocity, by collider handle (null: not a belt).\n- `explode(at: readonly [number, number, number] | Vector3, o: ExplosionOptions = {}): number`: Push every dynamic body near `at` away from it (physics/forces.ts). Returns how many.\n- `bind(body: RAPIER.RigidBody, object: Object3D): void`: Make `object` follow `body` (interpolated).\n- `unbind(object: Object3D): void`\n- `addStaticBox(o: BoxOptions): RAPIER.Collider`\n- `addStaticCylinder(position: [number, number, number], halfHeight: number, radius: number): RAPIER.Collider`\n- `addDynamicBox(o: BoxOptions): RAPIER.RigidBody`\n- `tag(collider: RAPIER.Collider, ...tags: string[]): RAPIER.Collider`: Tag a collider with gameplay semantics the character controller understands: 'climbable' (ladders/vines), 'pushable', 'grabbable', 'slippery', 'noLedge', 'character', 'noCamera' (camera rays pass through). Games may add their own.\n- `hasTag(collider: RAPIER.Collider | null | undefined, tag: string): boolean`\n- `raycast(from: Vector3, dir: Vector3, maxDistance: number, ignoreTags: readonly string[] = NO_TAGS): number | null`: Distance along `dir` (unit) from `from` to the first collider not carrying any of `ignoreTags`.\n- `castRay(from: Vector3, dir: Vector3, maxDistance: number, ignoreTags: readonly string[] = NO_TAGS, exclude?: RAPIER.RigidBody): { distance: number; collider: RAPIER.Collider; normal: Vector3; point: Vector3 } | null`: Ray cast returning distance, hit collider and surface normal.\n- `castUp(x: number, y: number, z: number, maxDistance: number, out: { y: number; nx: number; ny: number; nz: number; id: number }, ignoreTags: readonly string[] = NO_TAGS, exclude?: RAPIER.RigidBody): boolean`: Straight up from (x, y, z), at most `maxDistance` (a ceiling, a lift coming down): like `castDown`, `out.y` is the hit height.\n- `castDown(x: number, y: number, z: number, maxDistance: number, out: { y: number; nx: number; ny: number; nz: number; id: number }, ignoreTags: readonly string[] = NO_TAGS, exclude?: RAPIER.RigidBody): boolean`: Straight down from (x, y, z), at most `maxDistance`, skipping colliders with any of `ignoreTags` (pass a constant array) and `exclude`. Fills `out` (hit height, normal, collider handle) without allocating; \n- `groundBelow(origin: Vector3, maxDistance = 20, exclude?: RAPIER.RigidBody): { y: number; distance: number } | null`: Distance straight down from `origin` to the first collider (excluding `exclude`).\n- `trigger(shape: TriggerShape, at: readonly [number, number, number] | Vector3, options: TriggerOptions = {}): Trigger`: A trigger volume with enter/exit callbacks (pickups, checkpoints, kill zones, doors). Checked after every fixed step against dynamic and kinematic bodies (the character included); set `includeStatic` for level geometry. Never blocks movement or rays.\n- `updateTriggers(): void`: Run every trigger's overlap query now (Physics.update does this after each step).\n- `isAlive(target: { isValid(): boolean }): boolean`: Whether `target` (a body, collider or joint) still exists in the current world: false once removed, and for anything from a level before (its world is gone; `isValid()` would throw).\n- `remove(target: RAPIER.RigidBody | RAPIER.Collider): void`: Remove a rigid body (with its colliders and any bound Object3D binding) or a single collider. Removing a collider whose fixed body has no other colliders removes that body too (what `addStaticBox` & co. create). Tags and trigger overlaps are cleaned up.\n- `clear(): void`: Empty the world: every body, collider, joint, character and vehicle controller, trigger, tag, binding, mover, field and belt goes; gravity and the solver's iterations go back to their defaults, the step accumulator and the step count reset. `world` is a new R…\n- `fingerprint(): string`: A hash of the physics state: every body (position, rotation, velocities, asleep, enabled) and collider (pose, friction, restitution, sensor, enabled), exact, plus the step count and the time not yet stepped. Two runs that should be the same compare equal only…\n- `counts(): { bodies: number; colliders: number; joints: number; tags: number; bindings: number; triggers: number; controllers: number; vehicles: number; movers: number; fields: number; belts: number; }`: Sizes of everything Physics tracks (leak checks, debug UI).\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### interface LightRequestOptions\n\n- `position?: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]`: World position (copied). Ignored while `follow` is set (then it is `follow` + `offset`).\n- `follow?: Object3D | null`: Follow an object's world position every frame (a projectile, a monster, the hero).\n- `offset?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Offset from `follow` (world axes).\n- `color?: ColorRepresentation`\n- `intensity?: number`: Point light intensity at full strength (before flicker and fades). Default 4.\n- `radius?: number`: Reach in metres (PointLight.distance: light is 0 beyond it). Default 6.\n- `decay?: number`: Distance falloff exponent (PointLight.decay; a uniform, free to change). Default 1.2.\n- `flicker?: FlickerPreset`\n- `priority?: number`: Importance multiplier: the hero's light 4, a loot beam 2, a torch 1 (default).\n- `fadeIn?: number`: Fade times in seconds (defaults 0.25 in, 0.2 out).\n- `fadeOut?: number`\n- `lifetime?: number`: Release automatically after this many seconds (spell flashes, explosions).\n- `name?: string`: Optional label for debugging (`pool.describe()`).\n\n### interface LightHandle\n\nA light request. Change it freely; the pool decides whether it currently owns a real light.\n\n- `readonly id: number`\n- `readonly position: Vector3`: World position (mutable; ignored while following).\n- `readonly color: Color`: Colour (mutable).\n- `intensity: number`\n- `radius: number`\n- `decay: number`\n- `flicker: FlickerPreset`\n- `priority: number`\n- `follow: Object3D | null`\n- `readonly offset: Vector3`\n- `name: string`\n- `lifetime: number`: Seconds left before auto-release (Infinity = until `release()`).\n- `update(options: LightRequestOptions): this`: Set several fields at once.\n- `release(): void`: Fade out and give the light back. Safe to call twice.\n- `readonly alive: boolean`\n- `readonly lit: boolean`: Whether it holds one of the pool's real lights right now.\n- `readonly level: number`: 0..1: how much of it is shown (fades).\n\n### class Particles\n\nPixel-art particles: CPU-simulated pools, drawn as camera-facing square sprites in flat\npalette colors. One emitter (one instanced draw call, one pooled instance buffer) per\npreset; sizes are whole art pixels, so in Pixel mode a particle is a crisp N×N block.\nAvailable as `ctx.particles`; emitters are created on first use and freed by `clear()`\n(the engine calls it when a level unloads).\n\n  ctx.particles.burst('dust', hero.feet);\n  ctx.particles.burst('skid', feet, { direction: [-vx, 0.3, -vz], count: 2 });\n\n- `new Particles(view: () => ParticleView)`\n- `readonly group: Group<Object3DEventMap>`: Engine-owned group holding every emitter.\n- `readonly presets: Map<string, ParticlePreset>`: Preset registry: the built-ins plus anything registered.\n- `readonly pixelWorld: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`: World units per art pixel at the camera focus (updated every frame).\n- `register(name: string, preset: ParticlePreset): void`: Add or replace a named preset. Replacing frees the old preset's emitter.\n- `burst(preset: string | ParticlePreset, at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number], options: BurstOptions = {}): number`: Spawn a burst of `preset` (a name or a ParticlePreset) at `at`. Returns the number of particles spawned (fewer when the pool is full, 0 for an unknown preset). Inline preset objects share one emitter per distinct content, so `burst({ ...literal }, at)` every…\n- `get alive: number`: Particles alive right now, over all emitters.\n- `get emitterCount: number`: Emitters (= draw calls at most) currently allocated.\n- `update(dt: number): void`: Simulate and upload. Called by the engine every frame (frozen while paused).\n- `reseed(rng: Rng): void`: Take bursts' randomness from `rng` (the engine passes each level's `ctx.random` fork).\n- `clear(): void`: Kill every particle and free the emitters (GPU buffers, materials).\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n=== interface ParticleDef\\|interface ParticleConfig\\|interface EmitterConfig NOT FOUND\n### interface ClipDef\n\n- `name: string`\n- `frames: number`: Length in frames at 30 fps.\n- `loop?: boolean`\n- `keys: readonly Key[]`\n- `layers?: readonly Layer[]`\n- `feet?: readonly FeetKey[]`: Feet track: foot goals interpolated between its own keys and solved with IK every frame, overriding the keyed leg rotations. Use it whenever feet touch the floor through a transition (kneel → stand, sit → lie) so they roll instead of sinking.\n- `speed?: number`: Ground speed (m/s) the clip is authored for at playback rate 1. Locomotion clips set this; metrics then measure foot sliding against it and the runtime can scale playback.\n- `stance?: number`: Gait cycles (gaitClip): the share of the cycle each foot is planted. The right heel strikes at phase 0, the left at 0.5, so the right foot is mid-stance at `stance / 2`.\n- `reach?: number`: Gaits: how far ahead of the hips (m) the middle of the stance is (feet touch down `reach` + half a stance's ground ahead).\n- `grounded?: boolean`: Grounded clip: soles should rest on y = 0 (metrics flag floating / penetration).\n- `fast?: boolean`: Intentionally snappy (flips, punches, launches): skip the angular-speed warning.\n- `notes?: string`\n\n### compileClips()\n\n```ts\nfunction compileClips(defs: readonly ClipDef[], rig: RigSpec, model: Object3D): AnimationClip[]\n```\n\n### HERO_RIG\n\n```ts\nconst HERO_RIG: RigSpec\n```\n\nThe hero rig (public/assets/hero.glb, built by scripts/assets/hero.mjs).\n\nThe character faces +Z. L/R are the character's own sides (its right = -X).\nRotations are Euler XYZ degrees relative to rest; Z is applied first, then Y, then X,\nall about the parent's axes. Verified against contact sheets (`npm run anim -- sheet`).\n\n  Pelvis   +X tip forward (90 = face down, -90 = on its back, 360 = front flip)\n           +Y turn to its left, +Z roll to its RIGHT. p = [x, y, z] offset (m), s = squash\n  Torso    +X bend forward, +Y twist left, +Z lean right\n  Head     +X look down, +Y look left, +Z tilt right\n  Arm*     X swings: -90 = straight ahead, -180 = overhead, +40 = back\n           Z spreads (…\n\n## 14. three.js and Rapier\n\n### HERO_RAGDOLL\n\n```ts\nconst HERO_RAGDOLL: readonly RagdollPart[]\n```\n\nThe hero as a ragdoll (the joints of `hero.glb`, see rig.ts): pelvis, torso and head, upper\narms and legs on ball joints held in cones, elbows and knees as hinges bending the way the\nclips bend them (Forearm −X, Shin +X). Hands and feet ride on their forearm and shin.\n\n### skyAt()\n\n```ts\nfunction skyAt(hour: number): SkyState\n```\n\nThe sky at `hour` (wraps around 24).\n\n### applySky()\n\n```ts\nfunction applySky(engine: Pick<Engine, 'sun' | 'ambient' | 'scene' | 'setSunDirection'>, s: SkyState): void\n```\n\nPut a sky on the engine: sun direction, colour and strength, ambient light, background.\n\n### groundFog()\n\n```ts\nfunction groundFog(o: { color?: number; top?: number; amount?: number; near?: number; far?: number } = {}): { node: Node; color: UniformNode<\"color\", Color>; amount: UniformNode<\"float\", number>; top: UniformNode<\"float\", number>; }\n```\n\nFog for `scene.fogNode` with live settings (uniforms: changing them never recompiles):\nground mist that thickens below `top` (m) and a haze that grows with distance from the\ncamera, both up to `amount` (0..1), in `color` (match the sky).\n\n  const mist = groundFog({ color: s.sky, top: 1.5 });\n  (scene as { fogNode: unknown }).fogNode = mist.node;\n  mist.amount.value = 0.8; mist.color.value.setHex(s.sky);\n\n### gaitClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction gaitClip(rig: RigSpec, g: GaitSpec): ClipDef\n```\n\n### interface RigSpec\n\nEverything the tools need to know about a rig beyond its hierarchy.\n\n- `joints: readonly string[]`: Joint names in hierarchy order (root first).\n- `root: string`\n- `mirror: Readonly<Record<string, string>>`: Left/right counterparts for mirroring.\n- `soles: readonly string[]`: Mesh names that touch the ground (soles).\n- `trace: readonly string[]`: Points to trace in contact sheets (joint or mesh names).\n- `limits: Readonly<Record<string, JointLimit>>`: Human-plausible rotation ranges, degrees.\n- `fps: number`: Frame rate of the authoring format.\n- `legs?: LegRig`: Leg geometry, enabling foot IK and the gait generator.\n- `spine?: { torso?: string; head?: string; cap?: string }`: Joints the runtime procedural layers turn (PoseLayers): the torso and head look where the character is going; `cap` is an optional springy joint (not animated by clips).\n\n### renderSound()\n\n```ts\nfunction renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate = 44100, seed = 1): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>\n```\n\nRender a sound to mono samples in -1..1.\n\n### spatialize()\n\n```ts\nfunction spatialize(listener: Listener, at: V3Like, o: SpatialOptions = {}): Spatial\n```\n\nGain and pan of a sound at `at` for `listener`.\n\n### interface Voice\n\nA looping sound steered while it plays (`AudioManager.loop`): a hum, a fire, a waterfall.\n\n- `readonly name: string`\n- `readonly settings: Readonly<Required<VoiceSettings>>`: The values last set (kept headless too, so games and tests can read them).\n- `readonly sounding: boolean`: Really sounding: Web Audio running and the loop started.\n- `readonly stopped: boolean`\n- `set(s: VoiceSettings): void`: Change any of its settings; they glide over a few hundredths of a second (no clicks).\n- `stop(): void`\n\n### class FirstPersonRig\n\nEyes of the character: mouse look (click to lock the pointer) or Q/E to turn. No zoom.\n\n- `new FirstPersonRig(config: CameraConfig = {})`\n- `readonly preset: \"first\"`\n- `readonly camera: PerspectiveCamera`\n- `readonly zoomable: false`: Whether zoom applies (all presets except first person).\n- `readonly hidesTarget: true`: First person hides the player model.\n- `yaw: number`\n- `pitch: number`\n- `groundBasis(): { right: Vector3; forward: Vector3; }`: Screen-relative movement basis on the ground plane.\n- `describe(): { yaw: number; pitch: number; }`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n\n### class CameraRig\n\nBase rig: owns the three.js camera, zoom and the ground-plane movement basis.\n\n- `new CameraRig(config: CameraConfig)`\n- `readonly preset: CameraPreset`\n- `readonly camera: OrthographicCamera | PerspectiveCamera`\n- `readonly focus: Vector3`: World point the rig is centred on (used for shadow framing).\n- `zoom: number`\n- `readonly minZoom: number`\n- `readonly maxZoom: number`\n- `readonly zoomable: boolean`: Whether zoom applies (all presets except first person).\n- `readonly hidesTarget: boolean`: First person hides the player model.\n- `readonly lockDepth: boolean`: Side-scroller: characters should stay on their depth lane.\n- `controlsCharacter: boolean`: False while the free camera is flying (input drives the camera, not the player).\n- `snap: boolean`: Pixel-snap the camera (ortho only).\n- `aspect: number`: Viewport aspect (width / height), following the art resolution (see `setAspect`).\n- `rayAt(x: number, y: number, origin: Vector3, dir: Vector3): void`: The ray through screen point (x, y) in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up, as `input.pointer` gives them): where it starts (on the near plane) and its unit direction. For picking things with the mouse, or aiming at it. An orthographic rig stands far b…\n- `setZoom(zoom: number): void`\n- `setAspect(aspect: number): void`: Match the art resolution's aspect (adaptive framing changes it with the screen). `update()` calls this from `CameraUpdate.resolution`; the engine also calls it on swap.\n- `teleport(target: Vector3): void`: Place the camera without smoothing.\n- `groundBasis(): { right: Vector3; forward: Vector3 }`: Screen-relative movement basis on the ground plane.\n- `update(u: CameraUpdate): void`\n- `describe(): Record<string, unknown>`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n- `state(): CameraRigState`: The rig as data, exact (`engine.snapshot()`).\n- `setState(s: CameraRigState): void`: Put the rig back where `state()` found it (the same preset).\n\n### withUrlOptions()\n\n```ts\nfunction withUrlOptions(options: EngineOptions, search = location.search): EngineOptions\n```\n\nA game's own options with the review flags from the page URL (optionsFromUrl) on top:\n`Engine.start(game, withUrlOptions({ container, camera: { preset: 'side', zoom: 1.2 } }))`.\nThe camera is merged key by key (`?zoom=1.5` keeps the game's `side` preset), and\n`?filters=` replaces the game's look (a look would win over filters otherwise).\n\n## 2. Camera\n\n### class Rng\n\nSeeded randomness. Everything random in a game comes from an `Rng`, so the same seed always\ngives the same result (docs/DOCTRINE.md, principle 1). Each level gets one, `ctx.random`,\nseeded from the engine's seed and the game's name; fork it by purpose so streams stay\nindependent of how many numbers each one drew:\n\n  const level = ctx.random.fork(`level:${n}`);\n  const room = level.fork(`room:${i}`);   // independent of how many numbers `level` drew\n\nNever use Math.random in game or engine code (the lint refuses it).\n\n- `new Rng(seed: number | string)`\n- `get state: number`: The generator's whole state: save it, and set it back to draw the same numbers again.\n- `next(): number`: Uniform in [0, 1). mulberry32.\n- `fork(purpose: string | number): Rng`: A child generator for one purpose; does not advance this one.\n- `range(min: number, max: number): number`: Float in [min, max).\n- `int(min: number, max: number): number`: Integer in [min, max] (inclusive).\n- `chance(p: number): boolean`\n- `pick(items: readonly T[]): T`\n- `weighted(items: readonly T[], weight: (item: T) => number): T`: Pick by weight; entries with weight <= 0 never come up.\n- `shuffle(items: T[]): T[]`\n- `gaussian(mean = 0, sd = 1): number`: Normal distribution (Box–Muller).\n\n### swayObject()\n\n```ts\nfunction swayObject(root: Object3D, wind: WindUniforms, o: SwayOptions): void\n```\n\nMake every mesh under `root` sway (its materials replaced by swaying copies).\n\n### class WindUniforms\n\n- `readonly time: any`\n- `readonly wind: any`\n- `update(time: number, wind: Vector2 | readonly [number, number]): void`: Per frame (game time); `wind` along x and z (its length is the strength).\n\n### clothGrid()\n\n```ts\nfunction clothGrid(o: ClothOptions): VerletBody & { cols: number; rows: number; uv: Float32Array }\n```\n\nA sheet of cloth: a grid of particles with structural, shear and bend edges, two triangles per cell.\n\n### class SoftMesh\n\n- `new SoftMesh(body: VerletBody, material: Material, o: { uv?: Float32Array; doubleSided?: boolean } = {})`\n- `readonly body: VerletBody`\n- `sync(): void`: Copy the particles into the mesh (call once per frame after stepping the body).\n\n### interface WaterOptions\n\n- `size: readonly [number, number]`: Width (x) and depth (z) of the surface, m.\n- `at: readonly [number, number, number]`: Centre of the surface at rest.\n- `segments?: readonly [number, number]`: Grid quads along x and z (default: one per ripple cell, or 4 per metre without ripples).\n- `waves?: readonly Wave[]`\n- `ripples?: RippleField | null`: A ripple field covering the same rectangle (its size and centre must match).\n- `deep?: number`: Colours (hex): troughs, crests, foam.\n- `shallow?: number`\n- `foam?: number`\n- `foamAt?: number`: Height above rest (m) where foam starts. Default: 75% of the waves' combined height (the tallest crests).\n- `opacity?: number`: 1: opaque; less: see-through by a 4×4 ordered dither per art pixel.\n\n### interface SpriteOptions\n\n- `size?: number`: Height in metres (the width follows the frame's aspect). Default 1.\n- `frame?: number`\n- `flip?: boolean`: Mirror left-right (a sprite facing the other way).\n\n### spriteSheet()\n\n```ts\nfunction spriteSheet(idOrDef: string | TextureDef, opts: TextureOptions = {}): SpriteSheet\n```\n\nA sprite sheet for `SpriteBatch`: the def's frames left to right in one row, rows stored top\nfirst (`flipY` false, which SpriteBatch reads). Not cached: a SpriteBatch disposes its sheet.\n\n### textureDef()\n\n```ts\nfunction textureDef(def: T): T\n```\n\nReturns the def as written, typed, so tuples and literals don't widen (as `model` does). Not\n`texture`: TSL's `texture()` samples a map, and games import both.\n\n### class Trail\n\n- `new Trail(o: TrailOptions = {})`\n- `readonly capacity: number`\n- `width: number`\n- `life: number`\n- `readonly head: Color`: Head and tail colours (live: `trail.head.setHex(...)`).\n- `readonly tail: Color`\n- `push(p: Vector3, time: number): void`: The trail's newest point (call every frame while it moves).\n- `reset(): void`: Drop every point.\n- `get length: number`: Points alive now.\n- `update(time: number, camera: Camera): void`: Per frame: forget old points, rebuild the ribbon facing `camera`.\n- `dispose(): void`: Frees the GPU-related resources allocated by this instance.\n\n### class Decals\n\n- `new Decals(scene: Scene, o: { capacity?: number } = {})`\n- `readonly meshes: Map<\"ring\" | \"crack\" | \"footprint\" | \"scorch\" | \"splat\", InstancedMesh<BufferGeometry<NormalBufferAttributes, BufferGeometryEventMap>, Material<...> | Material…`\n- `readonly o: { capacity?: number }`\n- `add(shape: DecalShape, at: Vector3Like, normal: Vector3Like, o: DecalOptions = {}): number`: Lay a mark at `at` on a surface facing `normal`. Returns its index in that shape's pool.\n- `count(shape?: DecalShape): number`: Marks alive (per shape or all).\n- `update(dt: number): void`: Per frame: age the marks; old ones dither away over a second.\n- `clear(): void`: Remove every mark (the meshes stay for reuse).\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### class Boids\n\n- `new Boids(count: number, o: BoidOptions = {})`\n- `readonly count: number`\n- `readonly pos: Float32Array`\n- `readonly vel: Float32Array`\n- `o: Required<Omit<BoidOptions, 'bounds' | 'seed'>> & { bounds: BoidOptions['bounds'] }`\n- `seek: Vec3 | null`: A point everyone is drawn toward (null: none).\n- `seekWeight: number`\n- `flee: { at: Vec3; radius: number } | null`: A threat to scatter from.\n- `fleeWeight: number`\n- `obstacles: { x: number; y: number; z: number; r: number }[]`: Spheres to steer around (x, y, z, r).\n- `spacing(): number`: Average distance to the nearest neighbour (tests, tools).\n- `order(): { speed: number; alignment: number }`: Average speed and how lined up the headings are (0 random, 1 all the same way).\n- `step(dt: number): void`\n\n### class BehaviorTree\n\n- `new BehaviorTree(root: BtNode<B>, blackboard: B)`\n- `ticks: number`: Ticks so far.\n- `time: number`: Seconds ticked so far (the sum of every dt).\n- `status: Status | null`\n- `readonly root: BtNode<B>`\n- `readonly blackboard: B`\n- `tick(dt: number): Status`\n- `halt(): void`: Stop everything (a cutscene, a stun): running actions are told to halt.\n- `trace(): TraceRow[]`: Every node depth-first with this tick's answer: for drawing the tree.\n- `activePath(): string[]`: The names down the branch that decided this tick, root first (the last child each node ran that didn't fail).\n\n### bt\n\n```ts\nconst bt: { readonly selector: <B>(name: string, ...children: BtNode<B>[]) => BtNode<B>; readonly sequence: <B>(name: string, ...children: BtNode<B>[]) => BtNode<B>; ... 8 more ...; readonly cooldown: <B>(child: BtNode<...>, seconds: number, name?: string) => BtNode<...>; }\n```\n\nThe builders in one object, for `bt.selector(...)` without importing each.\n\n### interface TimelineOptions\n\n- `shots?: readonly CameraKey[]`\n- `cues?: readonly Cue[]`\n- `lines?: readonly Line[]`\n- `duration?: number`: Length (s); default the last key, cue or line.\n- `bars?: number`: Seconds the letterbox bars take to slide in and out (default 0.6; 0: no bars).\n- `onEnd?: () => void`: Called once when the playhead reaches the end (or the scene is skipped).\n\n### buildModel()\n\n```ts\nfunction buildModel(idOrDef: string | ModelDef, opts: ModelOptions = {}): ModelBuild\n```\n\n### addModelColliders()\n\n```ts\nfunction addModelColliders(physics: Physics, build: Pick<ModelBuild, 'colliders'>, at: Vec3 = [0, 0, 0], yaw = 0): RAPIER.Collider[]\n```\n\nStatic Rapier colliders for a placed model (its `colliders` data), at `at` turned `yaw`\nradians about Y: a box, an upright cylinder or a convex hull, with `addStaticBox`'s friction.\n\n### class ModelCatalog\n\nModels by id: the engine's built-in library (`MODEL_LIBRARY`) or a game's own. `use` parts\nand `buildModel('id')` resolve ids through one (the library unless a build passes another);\na game adds its models to the library, or builds a catalog of its own on top of it.\n\n- `new ModelCatalog(defs: readonly ModelDef[] = [])`\n\n=== interface BuildOptions\\|interface BuildModelOptions NOT FOUND\n### glowMaterial()\n\n```ts\nfunction glowMaterial(color: ColorRepresentation): MeshBasicNodeMaterial\n```\n\nUnlit, full-bright material for a colour (flames, eyes, screens, crystal cores), shared and\ncached per colour like `toonMaterial` (`userData.shared`; disposing it drops it from the\ncache). Its vertices snap with the toon materials' under the `ps1` filter, so a glowing part\nstays on its model.\n\n### interface ToonMaterialOptions\n\n- `map?: Texture | null`: Base color texture. Switched to nearest filtering (crisp texels, no mip blur).\n- `vertexColors?: boolean`: Multiply by the geometry's `color` attribute (vertex colors).\n- `rim?: number`: A hard-banded rim light in this colour (hex) on grazing, upward-facing surfaces: a silhouette that reads against dark floors (monsters in dark levels). Added to the emissive, so `material.emissive` (hit flashes) still works on top. Same shader for every rim c…\n- `dissolve?: boolean`: Pixel dissolve: the material reads its own `dissolve` property (0 = solid, 1 = gone) and discards a per-art-pixel noise pattern below it, with a 1-pixel glowing front in the rim colour (or white). Set it on per-body clones (`material.dissolve = 0.4`), not on…\n\n## 5. Physics\n\n### checkModel()\n\n```ts\nfunction checkModel(idOrDef: string | ModelDef, opts: CheckOptions = {}): ModelCheck\n```\n\nEvery check over the model's range: the defaults, then each param at its min and at its max\n(one at a time), each at seeds 1, 2 and 3 when the parts read the rng. Messages say which\nbuild they came from unless every build agrees.\n\n### validateTexture()\n\n```ts\nfunction validateTexture(def: TextureDef): TextureCheck\n```\n\n### mergeStaticMeshes()\n\n```ts\nfunction mergeStaticMeshes(sources: readonly Object3D[], options: MergeOptions = {}): Mesh[]\n```\n\nMerge static meshes into one mesh per (material, shadow flags): N blocks with M\nmaterials become ≤ M draw calls instead of N × groups (a BoxGeometry with 6 material\ngroups is 6 draws, plus 6 more in the shadow pass).\n\nWorld transforms are baked into the vertices, multi-material geometry is split by its\ngroups, and `position` + `normal` are kept, plus `uv` under a material with a `map` and\n`color` under one with `vertexColors` (what toon materials read). The\nsources are left untouched (don't add them to the scene). Physics is separate: keep\none collider per block. Merged meshes are culled as a whole, so merge things that are\non screen together (a level, a room), and keep anything that moves…\n\n### encodeState()\n\n```ts\nfunction encodeState(v: unknown): unknown\n```\n\nA deep copy of `v` as plain JSON that `JSON.stringify` keeps exactly: `-0`, `Infinity`,\n`-Infinity` and `NaN` become the strings `'@-0'`, `'@Infinity'`... (JSON would write 0 and null,\nand `atan2(-0, x)` is not `atan2(0, x)`), and a string of the game's own that starts with `@`\ngets one more (`'@x'` → `'@@x'`). `decodeState` turns them back. `engine.snapshot()` and\n`restore()` do both; a game's `snapshot()` returns its values as they are (never through JSON).\n\n### interface PrecipitationOptions\n\n- `kind: PrecipitationKind`\n- `count: number`: Most drops at once (the instance count).\n- `area?: readonly [number, number, number]`: Width, height and depth of the air the drops fall through, around the centre.\n- `color?: number`: Drop colour (hex).\n- `speed?: number`: Fall speed (m/s). Rain 16, snow 1.4.\n\n### class ScreenFx\n\n- `cover(kind: TransitionKind = 'fade', o: TransitionOptions = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Cover the picture (progress → 1). Resolves when it stops: `true` when it is fully covered, `false` when another transition (or `set`, `clear`) took over first.\n- `reveal(kind?: TransitionKind, o: TransitionOptions = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Uncover it (progress → 0) with `kind` (default: the one that covered it). Resolves like `cover`.\n- `transition(kind: TransitionKind, between: () => unknown, o: TransitionOptions & { revealCenter?: TransitionOptions['center'] } = {}): Promise<boolean>`: Cover, run `between` (load a level, teleport), then reveal, also when `between` throws (the error is passed on). Resolves to whether the cover completed.\n- `set(kind: TransitionKind | null, progress: number): void`: Jump straight to a progress (0 clear, 1 covered), no animation.\n- `flash(color: ScreenColor = 'white', o: FlashOptions = {}): void`: A full-screen flash that fades out.\n- `shockwave(at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number], o: ShockwaveOptions = {}): void`: A ring that pushes the picture outwards from `at` (oldest ring is replaced when 4 run).\n- `clear(): void`: Stop everything (a clear picture).\n- `state(): ScreenFxState`\n- `update(dt: number, camera: Camera | null): void`: Engine, every rendered frame (real seconds; `camera` projects world centres).\n- `sampleNode(tex: any, scale: any): any`: Sample an art-resolution picture for the device pass, pushed by the shockwaves and blocked up by a mosaic transition, with the rings' bright edge. `tex` is the art target (a texture node); `scale` is device pixels per art pixel.\n- `colorNode(color: any, scale: any): any`: The transition and the flash over a finished colour (device pass).\n\n### class CameraShake\n\n- `trauma: number`\n- `maxOffset: number`: World units at full trauma.\n- `decay: number`: Trauma lost per second.\n- `strength: number`: Global multiplier (settings: 0 turns shake off).\n- `readonly offset: Vector3`\n- `state(): ShakeState`: Trauma and the noise clock, as data (`engine.snapshot()`): what the next frames shake by follows from them.\n- `setState(s: ShakeState): void`\n- `add(amount: number): void`\n- `update(dt: number): Vector3`: Advance and compute `offset`.\n- `apply(rig: { readonly focus: Vector3 }, dt: number): void`: Update and move a camera rig's focus by the change in offset since the last call.\n- `reset(): void`\n\n","numLines":682,"startLine":1,"totalLines":682}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CNSL8tPyf9NcLQqQiFpNRD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
