{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkyiuvcFxYz5UMSYGS5X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Far Cry Uncle is built and handed in\n\nI built everything PLAN.md describes that can be done on this computer, and everything TASK.md asks for is in `work/`. All 1863 automated tests pass, but no person has played the game yet, so how it feels and how hard it is remain untested.\n\n## Checked against TASK.md and PLAN.md\n\n| What was asked | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| Single HTML in the root, named as specified | `FarCryUncle-FarCry2-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-162027.html`, 6.0 MB, loads no other files |\n| Dev files in a subfolder of the same name | Done; `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the kit's zip |\n| `exit-interview.json` beside the HTML | Parses, has exactly the requested fields, and every value is filled |\n| About/Credits screen and chat reply | All Source Information fields and every feedback question, in order, below and in the game |\n| Whole-game test | `node tests/run.mjs --all`: **1863 passed, 0 failed**, including a bot finishing both endings and all four hub scenarios |\n| Hand-in check | `node tools/handin.mjs`: **39 of 39 lines pass** |\n\nI re-read the plan after the first finish and found gaps, all now fixed:\n- **Missions:** the `steal`, `escort` and `ride` step types were missing.\n- **Driving:** the hands and gear lever were out of frame, and every vehicle shared one cab. Each now has its own wheel and dash.\n- **Fort Clement:** the walls weren't built from the library pieces.\n- **Sound:** nothing muffled sounds behind land or walls.\n- **Animation:** the holster clip and most stock clips never played, and nine of my clips pushed feet or knees through the floor.\n\n## What still hasn't been checked for real\n\nTASK.md allows this: \"Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\"\n\n- **Feel and difficulty:** the bot finishes the campaign using god mode and teleports, so that proves it can be completed, not that it's balanced. Software rendering here also runs at about 1 frame per second.\n- **Sound:** measured by signal level, never heard.\n- **Controller and touch:** tested with a stand-in pad and Chromium phone and tablet profiles, not real devices.\n- **iPhone:** the WebKit iPhone profile has no WebGPU, so the engine shows its failure message, with no uncaught errors, as the plan predicted. Real iOS Safari is untested.\n- **Multiplayer startup:** a second copy took about 170 s to start under software rendering, measured on an earlier run. Real GPUs should be much faster, but I can't confirm that here.\n\n## Where I differ from the plan\n\n- **Tuned values:** the fog numbers and M3's wind direction changed.\n- **Headquarters:** garrisons stay neutral until angered, and the hostile inner yard is smaller. Otherwise the contract givers' own guards shot you.\n- **Fever:** it doesn't cause collapses during M8, M9 or the ending.\n- **Fuel tanks:** moved off the road.\n- **Unused engine parts:** the stock `Push` and `Climb` clips (no ladders or doors in the plan), plus `gaitClip`, `SpringChain`, `Squash` and `twoBoneIK`, which were optional tools.\n\nNothing is committed to git, since you didn't ask for that.\n\n## Source Information\n\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 16:20:27\n- **Source:** Far Cry 2\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Open-world first-person shooter with survival pressure and systemic fire. First person on foot, at the wheel and in story scenes, with an optional chase camera in vehicles only.\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight kit, unedited), three.js r186 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.20, esbuild 0.28 to bundle one HTML file, Playwright 1.63 for tests, and our own JavaScript game systems on top.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native 3D-as-pixel-art pipeline at 480x270 with toon materials and its 16-colour palette. New people, guns, hands, vehicles, buildings and trees written as model data; library props for the generic things.\n- **Animation:** The engine's ClipDef system at 30 fps with compileClips and AnimationMixer. Hero clips for people, new keyed clips on the people rig and on a first-person hands rig (reloads, jams, four field surgeries), ragdolls, plus procedural sway and recoil.\n- **Audio:** All written in code: SoundDef synthesis for every effect, seven original Song tracks, generated Animalese-style gibberish voices per character. No audio files. Music defaults to 10%.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1 to 4 players, with its own card. On its own: solo with AI mercs, and two windows on one computer over BroadcastChannel.\n- **Testing:** Headless Node and Playwright Chromium with software WebGPU. One command prints failures and a one-line count. The engine's own check tool and model sheets. A stand-in pad, phone and tablet profiles, a WebKit iPhone check, two pages for a second player, all four hub scenarios. Screenshots only for what a script cannot check. No review subagents.\n- **Research:** None beyond the plan, the engine kit's docs and the AI Hub protocol.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n\n**How many issues did testing catch?**\n32 logged in tests/ISSUES.md, not counting my own mistakes in the test scripts.\n\n**How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?**\n17 of them: things like trucks that could never be repaired, a pause menu that closed itself, contract givers who shot you, and an ending that could strand you.\n\n**What was the most helpful test you ran?**\nThe playthrough bot: it plays M0 through both endings with real actions. It found most of the critical bugs. Second was a random-input soak with views on, which found a chase camera crash no script reached. The engine's clip checker earned its place too: run late, it found nine of my clips through the floor.\n\n**What was the least helpful test you ran?**\nThe static scans for forbidden words and font coverage. They never found a real bug, though they would have been embarrassing to skip.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n\n**What did you like most about the engine?**\nThe same game runs headless in Node far faster than real time, with real physics, so a bot could finish the whole campaign. Close second: 3D as pixel art gave the look at once, and models as data meant no art tools.\n\n**Did you get stuck or guess wrong?**\nYes. Fog with a negative top washed every surface toward the sky, because the mist term is a smoothstep from -top/2 to top. I found it by turning fog off. I also guessed wrong that one body part of the hands could stay a sibling of the gun, and rebuilt that rig. A torus size is a diameter, so a whole steering wheel and the hands on it sat out of frame. And collide: false on a nested model does not reach its parts, so one wall made over 4,000 physics boxes.\n\n**What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?**\nThe fire grid and everything hanging off it: painted scars, burned grass blades, swapped trees, flames, light and sound. After that, the first-person hands rig and the mission runner.\n\n**What was the hardest part about using the engine?**\nPosing first-person hands as numbers with a screenshot costing 25 seconds. Software rendering runs at about one frame a second, and several copies compiling shaders at once can take minutes.\n\n**What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)**\nNone. pixel-engine.js is byte-identical to the kit, and a test checks it.\n\n**If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?**\nMake first person a real mode: free use of Q and E, a look step you can extend, a pointer lock the game owns, and pad buttons kept apart from keyboard codes. Today each needs a workaround.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n\n**What did you like most about this prompt?**\nThe plan made the decisions and verified the traps, so effort went into the game. The list of engine facts saved hours, and the definition of done was concrete enough to test.\n\n**What was confusing?**\nGivers sit inside headquarters while the inner yard is hostile even with a pass, so I had to narrow the yard. The fog numbers were marked verified but washed the picture. And the source name in the file name has a space the template does not address; I dropped it.\n\n**What was the hardest thing to do?**\nJudging feel. I can prove a jam clears on the third press, but not that it feels good, because nothing here runs at real speed and nobody can listen.\n\n**What would you change in this prompt?**\nSay which numbers are starting points to tune rather than facts, and add a short list of moments to check by eye, so the screenshots budget goes to what matters most."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2074,"cache_read_input_tokens":237540,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2074},"output_tokens":735,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"71ec49de-27ff-45fb-a784-9f3cf302f915","uuid":"b812a840-ab0f-4328-85ed-b74f9a63849d","timestamp":"2026-11-06T03:39:39.218Z","request_id":"req_011CgkyiudkMhywhNLxEscZ2"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.]: Not clearly satisfied. The hand-in files are in place and tests pass, but the transcript does not show that all PLAN.md items doable on this machine are done. Known gaps remain: the plan's 'Done' checklist (section 27) asks that the game be finished by bot for both endings (the summary says this passed), and the transcript shows the final 'node tests/run.mjs --all' run completing with 1863 passed, 0 failed. However the transcript also shows the agent's own list of left-unused plan items (Push/Climb clips, gaitClip, SpringChain, Squash, twoBoneIK) and unverified items from the plan such as Math.random-free original content and the 'Every screen shows native prompts' checklist line, which the transcript never confirms item by item. Since the plan's completion is not demonstrated line by line, the condition cannot be confirmed from the transcript."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"71ec49de-27ff-45fb-a784-9f3cf302f915","uuid":"951ce8c2-9285-4a65-b3cf-89a83f79fa47","timestamp":"2026-11-06T03:39:45.200Z","isSynthetic":true}
