e About screen asks the prompt questions word for word, in order\nok   PLAN 27: credit \"Pixel Engine (MIT)\"\nok   PLAN 27: credit \"three.js (MIT)\"\nok   PLAN 27: credit \"Rapier (Apache-2.0)\"\nok   PLAN 27: credit \"The AI Hub session client\"\nok   PLAN 27: credit \"Inspired by Far Cry 2; not affiliated\"\nok   TASK 87: the delivered page contains \"Far Cry Uncle\"\nok   TASK 87: the delivered page contains \"Controller or keyboard required\"\nok   TASK 93: the delivered page carries the current About answers (rebuilt after the last edit)\nok   PLAN 27.1: the title is on the title screen (GAME_TITLE, drawn by the shell) and in <title>\nok   PLAN 27.2: runs from file:// with nothing beside it (browser suite asserts it)\nFAIL PLAN 27.3: tests/run.mjs --all is green for exactly these files (every suite stamp still matches; a failure would delete it) (9 passed, 0 failed, 8 suites unchanged)\nok   PLAN 27.4: all four hub scenarios, and the game outside a session\nok   PLAN 27.5: the bot finishes the game, both endings: both buddies alive to the column, one dead and alone\nok   PLAN 27.6: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs\nok   PLAN 27.7: every screen shows native prompts for PlayStation, Xbox and keyboard/mouse (systems suite walks them all; static holds every pad glyph to pixel art)\nok   PLAN 27.8: the plan's ten screenshots are newer than the last source change (and were looked at)\nok   PLAN 27.9: pixel-engine.js is byte-identical to the zip in vendor/, also compared in the static suite\nok   The delivered html was built after the last source change\n1 FAILED\n--- my processes\n(end)\n--- root\n4096 FarCryUncle-FarCry2-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-162027\n6042111 FarCryUncle-FarCry2-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-162027.html\n103129 PLAN.md\n3797 PROGRESS.md\n219 STAGE.md\n12600 TASK.md\n5887 exit-interview.json\nanimation: The engine's ClipDef system at 30 fps with compileClips and AnimationMixer. Hero clips for people, new keyed clips on the people rig and on a first-person hands rig (reloads, jams, four field surgeries), ragdolls, plus procedural sway and recoil, a generated limp (gaitClip), two-bone arms at the wheel (twoBoneIK), flags on a spring chain and a spring landing dip.\ntesting: Headless Node and Playwright Chromium with software WebGPU. One command prints failures and a one-line count. The engine's own check tool and model sheets. A stand-in pad, phone and tablet profiles, a WebKit iPhone check, two pages for a second player, all four hub scenarios. Screenshots only for what a script cannot check. No review subagents.\nT- 43 logged in tests/ISSUES.md, not counting my own mistakes in the test scripts.\nT- 18 of them: things like trucks that could never be repaired, a pause menu that closed itself, contract givers who shot you, and an ending that could strand you.\nT- The playthrough bot: it plays M0 through both endings with real actions. It found most of the critical bugs. Second was a random-input soak with views on, which found a chase camera crash no script reached. The engine's clip checker earned its place too: run late, it found nine of my clips through the floor. So did walking every screen in all three glyph sets, which found a Game Over with no prompt.\nT- The static scans for forbidden words and font coverage. They never found a real bug, though they would have been embarrassing to skip.\nE2- Yes. Fog with a negative top washed every surface toward the sky, because the mist term is a smoothstep from -top/2 to top. I found it by turning fog off. I also guessed wrong that one body part of the hands could stay a sibling of the gun, and rebuilt that rig. A torus size is a diameter, so a whole steering wheel and the hands on it sat out of frame. And collide: false on a nested model does not reach its parts, so one wall made over 4,000 physics boxes.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"71ec49de-27ff-45fb-a784-9f3cf302f915","uuid":"558f66ae-cb3d-46eb-a075-a57da0624e21","timestamp