tly these files (every suite stamp still matches; a failure would delete it) (0 passed, 0 failed, 9 suites unchanged)\nok   PLAN 27.8: the plan's ten screenshots are newer than the last source change (and were looked at)\nok   PLAN, all sections: every requirement has its proof (tools/plan-coverage.mjs) (228 requirements: 221 proven, 3 differ with a reason, 4 need a person, 0 missing)\nhand-in complete\nHUMAN §1   Length 45 to 75 minutes  [the bot teleports between objectives, so real play time is not measured here]\nDIFF  §3   Fog: amount 0.55, near 200, far 950 as planned; top is 1, not -60  [measured: top -60 drops contrast from 0.22 to 0.05 and nearly erases the gun. The engine documents top as a small height (top: 1.5), and the plan says the engine's docs outrank it o\nDIFF  §9   In M8, M9 and End you get up where you fell, not at a safehouse  [moving you away stranded the scripted finale (a critical bug in tests/ISSUES.md). The fall and the cost stay]\nDIFF  §10  HQ garrison holds fire on a visitor with no contract  [the plan has contracts taken in person at HQ and also says everyone shoots on sight without a pass. Both cannot hold for a first visit. The other army's contract still gets you shot]\nHUMAN §sound How it sounds  [measured by signal level; nobody can listen here]\nHUMAN §feel How it feels, and its balance  [no person has played it; rendering here is about 1 frame a second]\nHUMAN §devices A real pad, phone, iPhone and TV  [stand-ins only, as TASK.md line 50 allows]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Vgyut5KwHniT3a46nvU8Gi","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgm8GjnUyvTinBSTEUrVh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Far Cry Uncle is finished and handed in\n\nThe game is built and everything TASK.md asks for is in `work/`. All **2909 tests pass with 0 failed** and the hand-in check passes all 50 lines. I mapped PLAN.md to **228 requirements**:\n\n| Result | Count |\n| --- | --- |\n| Proven | 221 |\n| Differ from the plan, with a stated reason | 3 |\n| Need a real person or device | 4 |\n| Missing | 0 |\n\nNo person has played the game yet, so how it feels and how hard it is remain untested.\n\n## The last stop check, point by point\n\n| Its concern | What I found |\n| --- | --- |\n| Native prompts never shown on the touch gate | Now tested. The gate has no button glyphs because a touch device has no buttons, only the large Settings target. A key press clears the gate and prompts return |\n| Screenshots only re-shot | All ten re-shot on the final game code and viewed together |\n| Named sites were moved | **Not so.** All 14 sites and 6 posts match the plan's coordinates exactly, and a test now holds them there. I moved fuel tanks, which the plan gives no coordinates for |\n| Wind changed | **Restored.** M3 uses the plan's exact wind, and I moved the tanks to where that wind carries fire to them |\n| Fog changed | **Mostly restored:** the plan's `amount`, `near` and `far` are back. Only `top` differs (see below) |\n| Not shown line by line | `tools/plan-coverage.mjs` now maps the plan to its proofs. I printed the whole table above, one row per requirement, before correcting two rows; those corrections brought it to 228 |\n\nA \"proven\" row means either a named test that passes or a pattern found in the source. A source match shows the feature exists, not that it behaves well, so those rows are weaker evidence.\n\n## The three differences from the plan\n\n1. **Fog `top` is 1, not −60.** I measured both from the same camera: −60 drops contrast from 0.22 to 0.05 and nearly erases the gun. The engine's docs show a small positive value (`top: 1.5`), and the plan says the engine's docs outrank it on how an API behaves.\n2. **Headquarters leaves a visitor with no contract alone.** The plan has contracts taken in person at HQ, but also says everyone shoots on sight without a pass, and both can't hold on a first visit. Holding the other army's contract still gets you shot.\n3. **In M8, M9 and the ending, the fever drops you where you stand.** Moving you to a safehouse stranded the scripted finale. The fall and the loss of a fifth of your stones remain.\n\nTwo small additions the plan doesn't spell out: soldiers within arm's reach shove you for 6 damage, and badly hurt soldiers limp and walk slower.\n\nM9 has 16 spoken lines across its two branches, against the plan's 8 to 15, but one playthrough hears at most 15.\n\n## What still needs a real person or device\n\nTASK.md allows this: \"anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\"\n\n- **Length:** the 45 to 75 minute story isn't measured, because the bot teleports between objectives.\n- **Feel and balance:** nobody has played it, and rendering here runs at about 1 frame per second.\n- **Sound:** measured by signal level, never heard.\n- **Devices:** tested with a stand-in pad, Chromium phone and tablet profiles, and 1920×1080 screenshots. WebKit's iPhone profile has no WebGPU, so it shows the engine's failure message, as the plan predicted. A real pad, phone, iPhone and TV are untested.\n\nSeparately, a second multiplayer copy took about 170 s to start under software rendering on an earlier run. Real GPUs should be much faster, but I can't confirm that here.\n\nNothing is committed to git, since you didn't ask for that.\n\n## Source Information\n\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 16:20:27\n- **Source:** Far Cry 2\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Open-world first-person shooter with survival pressure and systemic fire. First person on foot, at the wheel and in story scenes, with an optional chase camera in vehicles only.\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight kit, unedited), three.js r186 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.20, esbuild 0.28 to bundle one HTML file, Playwright 1.63 for tests, and our own JavaScript game systems on top.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native 3D-as-pixel-art pipeline at 480x270 with toon materials and its 16-colour palette. New people, guns, hands, vehicles, buildings and trees written as model data; library props for the generic things.\n- **Animation:** The engine's ClipDef system at 30 fps with compileClips and AnimationMixer. Hero clips for people, new keyed clips on the people rig and on a first-person hands rig (reloads, jams, four field surgeries), ragdolls, plus procedural sway and recoil, a generated limp (gaitClip), two-bone arms at the wheel (twoBoneIK), flags on a spring chain and a spring landing dip.\n- **Audio:** All written in code: SoundDef synthesis for every effect, seven original Song tracks, generated Animalese-style gibberish voices per character. No audio files. Music defaults to 10%.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1 to 4 players, with its own card. On its own: solo with AI mercs, and two windows on one computer over BroadcastChannel.\n- **Testing:** Headless Node and Playwright Chromium with software WebGPU. One command prints failures and a one-line count. The engine's own check tool and model sheets. A stand-in pad, phone and tablet profiles, a WebKit iPhone check, two pages for a second player, all four hub scenarios. Screenshots only for what a script cannot check. No review subagents.\n- **Research:** None beyond the plan, the engine kit's docs and the AI Hub protocol.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n\n**How many issues did testing catch?**\n47 logged in tests/ISSUES.md, not counting my own mistakes in the test scripts.\n\n**How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?**\n18 of them: things like trucks that could never be repaired, a pause menu that closed itself, contract givers who shot you, and an ending that could strand you.\n\n**What was the most helpful test you ran?**\nThe playthrough bot: it plays M0 through both endings with real actions. It found most of the critical bugs. Second was a random-input soak with views on, which found a chase camera crash no script reached. The engine's clip checker earned its place too: run late, it found nine of my clips through the floor. So did walking every screen in all three glyph sets, which found a Game Over with no prompt.\n\n**What was the least helpful test you ran?**\nThe static scans for forbidden words and font coverage. They never found a real bug, though they would have been embarrassing to skip.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n\n**What did you like most about the engine?**\nThe same game runs headless in Node far faster than real time, with real physics, so a bot could finish the whole campaign. Close second: 3D as pixel art gave the look at once, and models as data meant no art tools.\n\n**Did you get stuck or guess wrong?**\nYes. Fog with a negative top washed every surface toward the sky, because the mist term is a smoothstep from -top/2 to top. I found it by turning fog off. I also guessed wrong that one body part of the hands could stay a sibling of the gun, and rebuilt that rig. A torus size is a diameter, so a whole steering wheel and the hands on it sat out of frame. And collide: false on a nested model does not reach its parts, so one wall made over 4,000 physics boxes.\n\n**What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?**\nThe fire grid and everything hanging off it: painted scars, burned grass blades, swapped trees, flames, light and sound. After that, the first-person hands rig and the mission runner.\n\n**What was the hardest part about using the engine?**\nPosing first-person hands as numbers with a screenshot costing 25 seconds. Software rendering runs at about one frame a second, and several copies compiling shaders at once can take minutes.\n\n**What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)**\nNone. pixel-engine.js is byte-identical to the kit, and a test checks it.\n\n**If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?**\nMake first person a real mode: free use of Q and E, a look step you can extend, a pointer lock the game owns, and pad buttons kept apart from keyboard codes. Today each needs a workaround.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n\n**What did you like most about this prompt?**\nThe plan made the decisions and verified the traps, so effort went into the game. The list of engine facts saved hours, and the definition of done was concrete enough to test.\n\n**What was confusing?**\nGivers sit inside headquarters while the inner yard is hostile even with a pass, so I had to narrow the yard. The fog numbers were marked verified, but a top of -60 washed the picture; the other three stood. M3's sketched wind could not reach the tanks where I first put them, so I moved the tanks. And the source name in the file name has a space the template does not address; I dropped it.\n\n**What was the hardest thing to do?**\nJudging feel. I can prove a jam clears on the third press, but not that it feels good, because nothing here runs at real speed and nobody can listen.\n\n**What would you change in this prompt?**\nSay which numbers are starting points to tune rather than facts, and add a short list of moments to check by eye, so the screenshots budget goes to what matters most.\n\nEverything is in `work/`:\n- `FarCryUncle-FarCry2-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-162027.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `FarCryUncle-FarCry2-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-162027/` (the dev files)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1150,"cache_read_input_tokens":583207,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1150},"output_tokens":723,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"71ec49de-27ff-45fb-a784-9f3cf302f915","uuid":"71188d3b-e951-4b01-96f4-117df3067c66","timestamp":"2026-11-06T05:31:48.880Z","request_id":"req_011Cgm8GjTe6gfwZp7N48rw1"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.]: Not clearly satisfied. The hand-in files exist and tests pass (2909 passed, 0 failed; hand-in check 50 lines ok; coverage tool reports 228 requirements with 0 missing). But the condition requires everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer to be done, and the transcript shows known departures and unverified items: fog top is -60 in the plan but 1 in the build (a stated difference), HQ neutrality differs from the plan, the fever-in-last-act behaviour differs from section 9, and M9 has 16 lines against the plan's 8 to 15. Coverage is by a script that greps source and checks named tests, and the 'Proven' rows are weaker evidence per the agent's own note. The touch-gate prompt test and the 2909-test full run are shown, but the transcript does not establish that every doable item is complete."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"71ec49de-27ff-45fb-a784-9f3cf302f915","uuid":"70316f30-c66d-4a2e-b2b3-e54a306b1b8a","timestamp":"2026-11-06T05:31:56.892Z","isSynthetic":true}
