{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgmCvh4o1nqCnpgpP6VoR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Far Cry Uncle: no change, and one plan value still differs on purpose\n\nI've changed nothing this round, and more passes won't change this. Here is where it stands, without overclaiming:\n\n- **What doesn't match:** fog `top` is 1, where PLAN.md line 147 says −60. That's the one known difference, and I'm telling you so rather than calling it a full match.\n- **What the check misread:** the HQ rule, the fever and the line counts were changed *to match* the plan. They aren't remaining differences: HQ soldiers now shoot without a pass, the fever collapse holds in every mission, and every mission has 8 to 15 spoken lines.\n- **Why I won't set −60 on my own:** the engine's formula gives flat mist over all ground above y = 30, and the plan's +40 height offset puts all the ground there. So −60 can't deliver the \"distance haze only\" that the same line asks for. It measurably wrecks the picture (contrast 0.05 against 0.22, gun nearly erased). The plan also says the engine's docs outrank it on how an API behaves. Changing a good game into a washed-out one just to pass a check wouldn't serve you.\n\n**This needs your decision.** The only way to get both values is to shift the whole rendered world below y = −60. I'm not doing that, because it's a risky rewrite of the world's coordinates just to make one number true.\n\nIf you read \"as written\" as literal, change `top` to `-60` at `src/world/sky.js:38` and run `node tools/build.mjs`, or tell me and I'll do it and rerun everything. I recommend leaving it at 1.\n\nThe rest stands from the last full run: 3168 tests passed, 0 failed, the hand-in check passes all 53 lines, and the coverage table shows 0 requirements missing. Four items still need a real person or device: play length, feel and balance, sound, and real hardware. Nothing is committed to git.\n\n## Source Information\n\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 16:20:27\n- **Source:** Far Cry 2\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Open-world first-person shooter with survival pressure and systemic fire. First person on foot, at the wheel and in story scenes, with an optional chase camera in vehicles only.\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight kit, unedited), three.js r186 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.20, esbuild 0.28 to bundle one HTML file, Playwright 1.63 for tests, and our own JavaScript game systems on top.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native 3D-as-pixel-art pipeline at 480x270 with toon materials and its 16-colour palette. New people, guns, hands, vehicles, buildings and trees written as model data; library props for the generic things.\n- **Animation:** The engine's ClipDef system at 30 fps with compileClips and AnimationMixer. Hero clips for people, new keyed clips on the people rig and on a first-person hands rig (reloads, jams, four field surgeries), ragdolls, plus procedural sway and recoil, a generated limp (gaitClip), two-bone arms at the wheel (twoBoneIK), flags on a spring chain and a spring landing dip.\n- **Audio:** All written in code: SoundDef synthesis for every effect, seven original Song tracks, generated Animalese-style gibberish voices per character. No audio files. Music defaults to 10%.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1 to 4 players, with its own card. On its own: solo with AI mercs, and two windows on one computer over BroadcastChannel.\n- **Testing:** Headless Node and Playwright Chromium with software WebGPU. One command prints failures and a one-line count. The engine's own check tool and model sheets. A stand-in pad, phone and tablet profiles, a WebKit iPhone check, two pages for a second player, all four hub scenarios. Screenshots only for what a script cannot check. No review subagents.\n- **Research:** None beyond the plan, the engine kit's docs and the AI Hub protocol.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n\n**How many issues did testing catch?**\n50 logged in tests/ISSUES.md, not counting my own mistakes in the test scripts.\n\n**How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?**\n19 of them: things like trucks that could never be repaired, a pause menu that closed itself, contract givers who shot you, and an ending that could strand you.\n\n**What was the most helpful test you ran?**\nThe playthrough bot: it plays M0 through both endings with real actions. It found most of the critical bugs. Second was a random-input soak with views on, which found a chase camera crash no script reached. The engine's clip checker earned its place too: run late, it found nine of my clips through the floor. So did walking every screen in all three glyph sets, which found a Game Over with no prompt.\n\n**What was the least helpful test you ran?**\nThe static scans for forbidden words and font coverage. They never found a real bug, though they would have been embarrassing to skip.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n\n**What did you like most about the engine?**\nThe same game runs headless in Node far faster than real time, with real physics, so a bot could finish the whole campaign. Close second: 3D as pixel art gave the look at once, and models as data meant no art tools.\n\n**Did you get stuck or guess wrong?**\nYes. Fog with a negative top washed every surface toward the sky, because the mist term is a smoothstep from -top/2 to top. I found it by turning fog off. I also guessed wrong that one body part of the hands could stay a sibling of the gun, and rebuilt that rig. A torus size is a diameter, so a whole steering wheel and the hands on it sat out of frame. And collide: false on a nested model does not reach its parts, so one wall made over 4,000 physics boxes.\n\n**What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?**\nThe fire grid and everything hanging off it: painted scars, burned grass blades, swapped trees, flames, light and sound. After that, the first-person hands rig and the mission runner.\n\n**What was the hardest part about using the engine?**\nPosing first-person hands as numbers with a screenshot costing 25 seconds. Software rendering runs at about one frame a second, and several copies compiling shaders at once can take minutes.\n\n**What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)**\nNone. pixel-engine.js is byte-identical to the kit, and a test checks it.\n\n**If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?**\nMake first person a real mode: free use of Q and E, a look step you can extend, a pointer lock the game owns, and pad buttons kept apart from keyboard codes. Today each needs a workaround.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n\n**What did you like most about this prompt?**\nThe plan made the decisions and verified the traps, so effort went into the game. The list of engine facts saved hours, and the definition of done was concrete enough to test.\n\n**What was confusing?**\nGivers sit inside headquarters while the inner yard is hostile even with a pass, so I had to narrow the yard. The fog numbers were marked verified, but a top of -60 washed the picture; the other three stood. M3's sketched wind could not reach the tanks where I first put them, so I moved the tanks. And the source name in the file name has a space the template does not address; I dropped it.\n\n**What was the hardest thing to do?**\nJudging feel. I can prove a jam clears on the third press, but not that it feels good, because nothing here runs at real speed and nobody can listen.\n\n**What would you change in this prompt?**\nSay which numbers are starting points to tune rather than facts, and add a short list of moments to check by eye, so the screenshots budget goes to what matters most."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3720,"cache_read_input_tokens":710030,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3720},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"71ec49de-27ff-45fb-a784-9f3cf302f915","uuid":"55a2f112-1f49-484f-afec-d0b78ca7c102","timestamp":"2026-11-06T06:33:22.413Z","request_id":"req_011CgmCvgiD7EWfoAbymaP7N"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.]: Not satisfied as written. The transcript shows PLAN.md line 147 specifies fog top: -60, but the build uses top: 1 (src/world/sky.js:38), and the assistant explicitly kept the deviation and recommended against the plan's value. The condition requires everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer to be done, so this known departure means the plan is not fully matched. Hand-in files and the 3168-test passing run are evidenced, but the plan-match requirement is not met."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"71ec49de-27ff-45fb-a784-9f3cf302f915","uuid":"23f508a8-70ee-45fd-8ff2-d5a551f73503","timestamp":"2026-11-06T06:33:24.739Z","isSynthetic":true}
